{"raw": {"https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Casual-Academics---Melbourne-Dental-School_JR-017697-1": {"title": "Casual Academics - Melbourne Dental School", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-21\nVariable time\n\nRole type: \u00a0 Casual; Sessional academic \u00a0 Faculty: \u00a0 Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences \u00a0 Department / School: \u00a0 Melbourne Dental School \u00a0 Salary: \u00a0 Casual \u00a0 sessional \u00a0 academic rates as per University of Melbourne Enterprise Agreement, plus \u00a0 1 2 % super \u00a0 \u00a0 Shape the next generation of \u00a0 oral health practitioners by \u00a0 teaching \u00a0 \u00a0in \u00a0 the \u00a0 D octor of Dental Surgery (D DS ) , \u00a0 Bachelor of Oral Health ( BOH ) , and Doctor of Clinical Dentistry (DCD) \u00a0 program s \u00a0 at \u00a0 Melbourne Dental \u00a0 School \u00a0 / Enjoy flexible, sessional academic work. Apply now to grow your clinical teaching \u00a0 expertise \u00a0 while guiding safe, evidence-based dental care for diverse patients \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to \u00a0 benefit \u00a0 society through the transformative impact of education and research. \u00a0 Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity \u00a0 \u00a0 You will play a key role in developing \u00a0 dental and oral health therapy \u00a0 students\u2019 clinical competence and professional judgement. \u00a0 You will supervise preclinical and clinical activities at \u00a0 the \u00a0 Melbourne Dental School , guiding safe, high-quality care for diverse patients. \u00a0 You will also build your own teaching skills within a collaborative, \u00a0 values -driven dental school community. \u00a0 \u00a0 What you will deliver: \u00a0 \u00a0 Prepare for preclinical and clinical teaching sessions, reviewing materials, patient \u00a0 records \u00a0 and \u00a0 logistics \u00a0 to support \u00a0 evidence based \u00a0 learning. \u00a0 Supervise, \u00a0 guide \u00a0 and educate \u00a0 students during preclinical and clinical sessions, focusing on skills acquisition and knowledge application. \u00a0 Review and approve patient dental records and \u00a0 clinic \u00a0 \u00a0documentation , ensuring \u00a0 accurate , compliant clinical records for every session. \u00a0 Facilitate student feedback discussions and complete required assessment, marking, \u00a0 questionnaires \u00a0 and sessional administrative documentation. \u00a0 Participate in meetings, \u00a0 training \u00a0 and compliance activities to \u00a0 maintain \u00a0 credentialing, child \u00a0 safety \u00a0 and high-quality teaching standards. \u00a0 \u00a0 You may be a great fit \u00a0 if \u00a0 you : \u00a0 \u00a0 Hold a primary dental qualification, eligible for registration with the Dental Board of Australia. \u00a0 Bring at least \u00a0 two \u00a0 years\u2019 clinical experience \u00a0 (varies between programs) , with strong, contemporary evidence-based dental practice skills. \u00a0 Maintain current \u00a0 Ahpra \u00a0 registration with no conditions, and \u00a0 clinic \u00a0 credentialing \u00a0 appropriate to \u00a0 clinical teaching \u00a0 on the MDS teaching clinics. Demonstrate strong motivation to develop excellence in dental education, \u00a0 assessment \u00a0 and student feedback. \u00a0 Work effectively both independently and collaboratively, contributing positively to diverse students, \u00a0 patients \u00a0 and academic colleagues. \u00a0 Exhibit well-developed communication and interpersonal skills, supporting students\u2019 learning in complex preclinical and clinical environments. \u00a0 For further \u00a0 information \u00a0 please refer to the attached \u00a0 job duties \u00a0 description . \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Your new team \u2013 \u00a0 Melbourne Dental School \u00a0 At the Melbourne Dental School, you will join a values-driven community within the Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences. The \u00a0 School \u00a0 partners closely with the Royal Dental Hospital of Melbourne \u00a0 (RDHM) , delivering high-impact clinical education and patient care. You will collaborate with skilled clinicians and educators who champion evidence-based practice, \u00a0 innovation \u00a0 and student success, while enjoying \u00a0 strong support \u00a0 for your professional development and wellbeing. \u00a0 \u00a0 Be \u00a0 y ourself \u00a0 \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe \u00a0 organisation . The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. \u00a0 Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're \u00a0 committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. \u00a0 Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process . \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander \u00a0 a pplicants \u00a0 \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. \u00a0 Visit \u00a0 our \u00a0 Indigenous staff page \u00a0 to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at \u00a0 korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . \u00a0 \u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \u00a0 \"apply with us\" \u00a0 to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. \u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. \u00a0 A Working with Children Check is \u00a0 required \u00a0 for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0 \u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.\u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this specific position, contact \u00a0 mds-operations@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 \u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email \u00a0 hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at \u00a0 hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). \u00a0 \u00a0 Apply \u00a0 today, and \u00a0 join a community \u00a0 that\u2019s \u00a0 shaping the future. \u00a0 Job\u00a0Duties\u00a0Description\u00a0\u2013 Doctor of Dental\u00a0Surgery\u00a0(DDS).pdf Job \u00a0 Duties \u00a0 Description \u2013 Bachelor of Oral Health \u00a0 (BOH) \u00a0 ( Coming \u00a0 Soon) \u00a0 Job \u00a0 Duties \u00a0 Description \u2013 Doctor of \u00a0 Clinical \u00a0 Dentistry \u00a0 (DCD) \u00a0 ( Coming \u00a0 soon )"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Expressions-of-Interest--Indigenous-Employment-Opportunities-at-the-University-of-Melbourne_JR-006839": {"title": "Expressions of Interest: Indigenous Employment Opportunities at the University of Melbourne", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-04\nFull time\n\nThe University of Melbourne is inviting Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates to submit an Expression of Interest (EOI) for current and future opportunities across both professional and academic positions. Whether you are just starting your career or seeking the next step in your professional or academic journey, we encourage you to express your interest in joining our vibrant and diverse community. You may express interest in the following professional areas: Finance | Call Centre & Customer Service | Student Support Services | HR, Recruitment & Culture | Legal | Fundraising | Administration & Office Support | Management Roles | Strategy, Culture & Analytics | Information & Communications Technology | Marketing & Communications You may express interest in the following academic roles across all faculties: Tutor | Research Assistant | Research Fellow | Lecturer | Associate Professor Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as these positions are exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic).\u202f The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfillment, including:\u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance, including Cultural Leave\u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0with access to Indigenous counsellors for Indigenous staff Dedicated and ongoing support from Korin Korin, our Indigenous Employment and Development team A vibrant and active Indigenous Staff Network, providing opportunities for connection and professional development For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. We believe the diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing reasonable adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. For assistance, please contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Contact details for other enquiries are located at the bottom of this page. Find more information for applicants requiring reasonable adjustments. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. outlines our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff and students. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin Korin, at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au for further information and support.\u00a0 Our Indigenous strategy, Murmuk Djerring outlines our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff and students. \u00a0 Please note : This role requires current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available. \u00a0 A Working with Children Check (WWCC) is required for all positions. If successful, we'll guide you through the WWCC application process during onboarding and reimburse the cost. Join Us and unlock your career potential! \u00a0 If this role is right for you, please apply with the following documents:\u00a0 Resume \u00a0 Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience Proof of Aboriginality Please note: As part of the application, you\u2019ll be asked to share your preferences for the types of roles and levels of experience that best match your background. For recruitment queries, email Olivia korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Include the Position Number and Job Title in the subject line. Please do not send applications to this email address."}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Early-Childhood-Educator-s---Teacher-s--Casual-Positions_JR-016427": {"title": "Early Childhood Educators & Teachers, Casual Positions", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-07\n\nPlease apply via this link: https://careers.giighire.com/universityofmelbourne/29027?source=link DO NOT APPLY VIA THE UOM CAREERS PAGE Role type: Casual positions Department : Student and Scholarly services Support high-quality early learning / collaborate in inclusive, research-informed practice / join a respected University childcare service Apply now to contribute your expertise in early childhood education across our vibrant University children\u2019s centres. Investing in you \u2013 health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity You will support high-quality early childhood programs across our University centres, grounded in evidence-based pedagogy. You will collaborate with experienced colleagues to create inclusive, play-based learning that strengthens every child\u2019s wellbeing. You will gain varied experience supporting different age groups, rooms, and services within a child-safe organisation. \u00a0 What you will deliver: Plan, implement, and evaluate play-based learning programs aligned with EYLF, VEYLDF, and service philosophies. Document children\u2019s learning, development, and wellbeing to inform individual planning and continuous programme improvement. Maintain safe, inclusive environments through active supervision, risk management, and adherence to all regulatory requirements. Build strong, respectful relationships with children, families, and colleagues to support belonging and collaborative practice. Collaborate with room leaders and teams to reflect on practice and support service quality improvement. \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: Hold an approved Early Childhood degree and current WWCC or VIT registration. Demonstrate sound knowledge of NQF, National Law and Regulations, and Victorian Child Safe Standards. Bring proven experience delivering high-quality education and care using EYLF, VEYLDF, and evidence-based pedagogies. Create inclusive, stimulating environments that promote children\u2019s agency, participation, and strong sense of belonging. Apply diligent risk management, active supervision, and child-safe practices in dynamic early childhood settings. Adapt flexibly across rooms, age groups, and teams while maintaining professional, collaborative conduct. \u00a0 For further information please refer to the attached PD. \u00a0 What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program \u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page! \u00a0 Your new team \u2013 Student and Scholarly Services Student and Scholarly Services supports students and staff from first enquiry through to graduation and beyond. Early Childhood Education Services operates three high-quality University children\u2019s centres, delivering evidence-based programmes for infants to preschoolers. You will join a collaborative, research-informed team committed to child safety, inclusion, and continuous quality improvement. \u00a0 Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . \u00a0 Application essentials: Please ensure to apply through this link We will NOT be accepting applications through the UOM Careers Page You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience to the link provided as part of the application process. Please apply via this link: https://careers.giighire.com/universityofmelbourne/29027?source=link DO NOT APPLY VIA THE UOM CAREERS PAGE \u00a0 Position Description: Early Childhood Educator\u2019s & Teacher\u2019s - Casual Positions.docx Early Childhood Educator\u2019s & Teacher\u2019s - Casual Positions.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Lecturer--Indigenous-Social-Equity-and-Leadership---Expression-of-Interest_JR-014395": {"title": "Lecturer, Indigenous Social Equity and Leadership - Expression of Interest", "description": "Posted: 2026-06-10\nFull time\n\nThe University of Melbourne is inviting Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates to submit an Expression of Interest (EOI) for a lecturer, Indigenous Social Equity and Leadership position on a full-time continuing basis. This opportunity is offered at Academic Level B, with a salary range of $124,656\u2013$148,023 per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is\u00a0exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the\u00a0Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic). \u00a0 Your next career opportunity: \u00a0 You will design,\u00a0teach\u00a0and assess postgraduate subjects in Social Change Leadership for diverse experienced Fellows. You will advance research on Indigenous social\u00a0change while supporting Fellows\u2019 community-led projects and long-term leadership journeys. You will collaborate across the University and global Atlantic Fellows network to grow your academic and impact profile.\u00a0 What you will deliver: \u00a0 Design, deliver and coordinate engaging postgraduate subjects, using culturally grounded, flexible teaching approaches for adult learners.\u00a0 Supervise and support Fellows to develop rigorous social change project proposals with strong community partnerships and ethical foundations.\u00a0 Conduct and publish high-quality research on Indigenous social change and leadership, securing external funding where\u00a0appropriate.\u00a0 Contribute to AFSE\u2019s research,\u00a0seminars\u00a0and public engagement,\u00a0representing\u00a0AFSE locally,\u00a0nationally\u00a0and internationally.\u00a0 Build and sustain collaborations with Indigenous communities,\u00a0partners\u00a0and University colleagues to strengthen AFSE\u2019s teaching and impact.\u00a0 \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a doctoral qualification, or be near completion, in a field related to Indigenous social change.\u00a0 Demonstrate experience co-designing curriculum that embeds Indigenous knowledges, cultural safety principles and decolonial teaching practices.\u00a0 Show a strong record, or clear potential, in research, publication and collaborative project development.\u00a0 Exhibit excellence in teaching adult learners, including experienced leaders, using inclusive and reflexive pedagogical approaches.\u00a0 Work effectively within small, interdisciplinary teams, contributing constructively to programme development and shared strategic priorities.\u00a0 Engage confidently with Indigenous communities and partners, nurturing ethical, long-term relationships that support social change.\u00a0 Your new team: \u00a0 Atlantic Fellows for Social Equity You will join the Atlantic Fellows for Social Equity, a collaborative programme advancing Indigenous\u00a0and Pacific-led social change. AFSE combines a rich Foundation\u00a0year of\u00a0fellowship\u00a0and\u00a0postgraduate study\u00a0while\u00a0nurturing\u00a0a lifelong community of senior Fellows. The team works closely with communities,\u00a0partners\u00a0and a global Atlantic Fellows network to amplify impact. You will collaborate with committed colleagues across the University,\u00a0including the Faculty of Education,\u00a0benefiting\u00a0from strong mentoring and development opportunities.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u00a0\u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences.\u00a0 Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at\u00a0UniMelb. \u00a0 We're\u00a0committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities.\u00a0 Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0\u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians.\u00a0Visit\u00a0our\u00a0 Indigenous staff page \u00a0to learn more about\u00a0our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at\u00a0 oied-hr@unimelb.edu.au .\u00a0\u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit\u00a0 \"apply with us\" \u00a0to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.\u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0\u00a0 A Working with Children Check is\u00a0required\u00a0for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application. Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this expression of interest, please contact\u00a0Program Director,\u00a0Dr. Yvonne\u00a0Ualesi\u00a0at\u00a0yvonne.ualesi@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Position Description: PD_JR-014395 EOI Lecturer Indigenous Social Equity and Leadership.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Associate-Professor-in-Periodontics_JR-009367-1": {"title": "Professor in Periodontics", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-10\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Part-time (0.8 FTE) to Full-time; Continuing Faculty: \u00a0Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences School: \u00a0Melbourne Dental School Salary: Level D: AUD $183,859 - $202,556 p.a. \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Level E: AUD $236,824 p.a. \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 plus 17% super and clinical loading of AUD $13,494 p.a. International leadership in specialist Periodontics / Shape a nationally benchmarked Doctor of Clinical Dentistry (DCD) Periodontics program / Drive impactful translational research Join us to lead specialist Periodontics education, research and clinical innovation that advances oral health globally. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will lead the Doctor of Clinical Dentistry (DCD) Periodontics program, integrating specialist clinical training, cutting-edge research and curriculum innovation. You will shape national and international standards in Periodontics while mentoring the next generation of specialist clinicians. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Lead the design, delivery and evaluation of the DCD Periodontics curriculum and assessment across clinical and academic components. Provide high-quality clinical supervision and safe patient care at RDHM, modelling evidence-based, multidisciplinary specialist Periodontics practice. Establish and lead an internationally recognised research program in Periodontics, securing competitive funding and collaborative partnerships. Supervise and mentor higher degree and postgraduate research students, building research capacity across the discipline and School. Provide strategic leadership in national curriculum frameworks, accreditation standards and professional guidelines for specialist Periodontics. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold and maintain current or clearly eligible specialist AHPRA registration in Periodontics, without limiting conditions or restrictions. Possess a completed PhD in Periodontics or a closely related field, with strong academic credentials. Demonstrate strategic leadership in specialist clinical teaching, including curriculum design, assessment, benchmarking and educational innovation. Show a sustained record of high-quality research, including competitive grant success and impactful translational outcomes. Bring extensive experience supervising higher degree and postgraduate research students through to successful thesis completion. Engage confidently with national and international professional bodies, accreditation panels and multidisciplinary clinical partners. For further information please refer to the attached Candidate Information Pack .\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 \u201cMelbourne Dental School\u201d \u00a0 You will join the Melbourne Dental School, a leader in specialist dental education and translational research. Working closely with the Royal Dental Hospital of Melbourne, you will help deliver world-class patient-centred clinical training. You will collaborate in a values-driven Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences, committed to Advancing Health 2030. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Professor Petros Papagerakis or Professor Ivan Darby at the details listed in the position description. For questions related to our recruitment process email Shabon Nawabi at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Friday 9th October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 Candidate Information Pack - Professor in Periodontics.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Senior-Partnership-and-Engagement-Manager--MEC_JR-018267": {"title": "Senior Partnership and Engagement Manager, MEC", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term (2 years) \u00a0 Department: Melbourne Entrepreneurial Centre, Melbourne Enterprise \u00a0 Salary: UOM 9 - $146,049 - $151,951 p.a. plus 17% super \u00a0 Senior leadership role in a growing entrepreneurial centre / build strategic corporate and philanthropic partnerships / shape MEC 2030 impact \u00a0 Join MEC to lead partnerships, community engagement and events that fuel Melbourne\u2019s entrepreneurial ecosystem and venture success. \u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. \u202f \u00a0 \u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u202f \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity: \u00a0 In this senior role, you will lead MEC\u2019s partnerships, community and events functions to drive sustainable growth. \u00a0 You will cultivate corporate and philanthropic partners while energising a vibrant network of students, alumni, mentors and innovators. \u00a0 What you will deliver: \u00a0 Lead MEC\u2019s commercial and philanthropic partnerships strategy, pipeline and portfolio to grow revenue and strategic relationships. \u00a0 Build and activate MEC\u2019s community, alumni and mentor networks to support programs and entrepreneurial engagement. \u00a0 Oversee a high-quality events program that showcases MEC, strengthens partnerships and deepens community connections. \u00a0 Provide inclusive leadership, clear direction and coaching to partnerships and engagement staff within a growing team. \u00a0 Monitor performance, budgets and risk, delivering regular reports on revenue, engagement metrics and partnership impact. \u00a0 \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Demonstrate a strong record securing corporate and philanthropic partnerships with measurable revenue and relationship outcomes. \u00a0 Apply commercial acumen to build and manage partnership pipelines, forecasts and stakeholder reporting in complex organisations. \u00a0 Design and deliver stakeholder, community or alumni engagement programs that deepen long-term participation and advocacy. \u00a0 Lead and coach professional staff, fostering collaborative, high-performance cultures aligned with shared goals. \u00a0 Plan and deliver impactful events, from intimate partner engagements to large-scale showcases, with excellent participant experiences. \u00a0 Engage confidently with entrepreneurship and start-up ecosystems, using data and insights to improve programs and partnerships. \u00a0 \u00a0 What we offer you! \u202f \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: \u202f \u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation \u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being \u202f \u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning \u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance \u202fand well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave \u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company \u202f \u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program \u202f \u00a0 \u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page! \u202f \u00a0 Your new team: \u00a0 Melbourne Entrepreneurial Centre (MEC) is the University\u2019s hub for entrepreneurship, supporting students, researchers and alumni to build ventures. \u00a0 MEC sits within Melbourne Enterprise and Melbourne Research and Enterprise, connecting deeply with faculties and industry partners. \u00a0 Based at Melbourne Connect, you will join a collaborative team scaling programs, partnerships, community and impact. \u00a0 Be Yourself \u202f \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb . \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process . \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u202f \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .\u202f \u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. \u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0 \u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0 \u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this specific position, contact Monica Bulla-Valencia \u00a0 at monicab@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at Alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 \u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. \u00a0 Applications close: \u200b Sunday 11 October 2026 - 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u202f \u00a0 Position Description: \u202f \u00a0 PD_JR-018267 Senior Partnership &amp; Engagement Manager, MEC.pdf PD_JR-018267 Senior Partnership &amp; Engagement Manager, MEC.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Werribee/Equine-Veterinary-Nurse_JR-017945-1": {"title": "Equine Veterinary Nurse", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Full-time; Continuing Faculty: \u00a0Science Department/School: \u00a0Melbourne Veterinary School \u2013 Werribee Equine Centre Salary: \u00a0UoM 4: $80,400 - $85,328 p.a. plus 17% super Renowned equine teaching hospital environment / varied hospital and ambulatory caseload / strong focus on education and growth Step into a leading equine teaching hospital and grow your nursing career supporting complex, meaningful cases. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 As an Equine Veterinary Nurse, you will provide high quality care in hospital and ambulatory settings. You will support specialist clinicians, mentor DVM students and contribute to a leading equine teaching hospital. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Provide high quality equine nursing care, including horse handling, restraint, treatment support and ongoing inpatient husbandry. Assist veterinarians with diagnostics, surgery, anaesthesia, and emergency care, ensuring thorough preparation and post procedure monitoring. Maintain clinical equipment, stocks, and records, upholding rigorous biosecurity, infection control and workplace safety standards. Support delivery of ambulatory services by preparing vehicles, equipment and providing nursing assistance during farm visits. Guide DVM students in practical equine nursing, horse handling and professional behaviour within a supportive teaching environment. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a Diploma or Certificate IV in Veterinary Nursing, or equivalent experience, ideally with equine focus. Bring proven equine veterinary nursing and horse handling experience within a clinical practice or hospital environment. Demonstrate strong organisational skills, with ability to manage competing priorities and follow detailed clinical protocols. Apply sound knowledge of animal welfare, infection control, biosecurity and safe large animal handling practices. Use veterinary practice software and animal care databases confidently to support accurate records and billing. Value working in a collaborative teaching hospital, supporting veterinarians and mentoring veterinary students. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 Melbourne Veterinary School Melbourne Veterinary School, in the Faculty of Science, is a highly ranked leader in veterinary education. The Werribee Equine Centre delivers specialist referral, primary care and ambulatory services as a leading equine teaching hospital. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Carina Wickens (Details in Position Description) For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: 19 October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 JR-017945 Equine Veterinary Nurse_PD.pdf JR-017945 Equine Veterinary Nurse_PD.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Academic-Positions--Level-B--C-or-D--Education---Research-Indigenous-Law-and-Justice-Hub_JR-018041-1": {"title": "Academic Positions (Level B, C or D) Education & Research Indigenous Law and Justice Hub", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Full time, Continuing Faculty: Melbourne Law School / Indigenous Law and Justice Hub Salary: UoM B - $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super UoM C - $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. plus 17% super UoM D - $183,859 - $202,556 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Champion Indigenous legal scholarship and advocacy / shape culturally safe curricula and research partnerships / collaborate with First Nations scholars Apply now to help redefine legal education and research through Indigenous knowledge, leadership and community partnerships. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic). \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 Melbourne Law School invites applications for an academic position within the Indigenous Law and Justice Hub. The level of appointment will be commensurate with the successful applicant\u2019s qualifications, experience and academic profile. The successful applicant will contribute to the School\u2019s teaching, research, engagement, leadership and service, bringing expertise in any area of law relevant to the work of the Indigenous Law and Justice Hub. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Teaching in the Melbourne Juris Doctor, Melbourne Law Masters and, where appropriate, undergraduate Breadth programs. Contributing to curriculum and subject development, particularly in relation to law and Indigenous peoples. Supervising coursework and/or higher degree research students, as appropriate to level and experience. Undertaking high-quality, nationally and internationally significant legal research and publishing in leading outlets. Contributing to research projects, grant activity and knowledge exchange. Supporting an engaging, inclusive and high-quality student learning experience. Building relationships and partnerships through the Indigenous Law and Justice Hub and contributing to community and professional engagement. Contributing to the collegial life, leadership and service of Melbourne Law School and the University, with more senior appointments expected to make a significant contribution to Indigenous strategy and cultural capability. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 A strong academic background in law and relevant postgraduate qualifications, including a PhD or equivalent (or equivalent professional experience where applicable). A developing, established or internationally recognised record of legal research and publication, commensurate with level. Teaching experience and a commitment to high-quality legal education and student outcomes. The capacity to contribute to research, engagement, leadership and service. A commitment to collegiality, diversity and inclusion, and maintaining a culturally safe workplace. Relevant professional experience, networks and/or expertise in legal education and educational technologies will be highly regarded. Applicants should identify the academic level for which they are applying and demonstrate their suitability against the relevant criteria. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 Melbourne Law School The Indigenous Law and Justice Hub\u00a0brings\u00a0together Indigenous leaders, legal\u00a0scholars\u00a0and communities to advance justice. Based within Melbourne Law School, the Hub embeds Indigenous knowledges across teaching, research,\u00a0engagement\u00a0and student support. You will join a\u00a0values\u2011driven,\u00a0empathetic\u00a0and collaborative team committed to cultural safety and\u00a0community\u2011led\u00a0priorities.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Nicole Watson at nicole.watson.1@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 18 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: MLS Academic Recruitment 2026 Indigenous PD.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Research-Fellow---Senior-Research-Fellow_JR-018549": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow / Research Fellow / Senior Research Fellow", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Full time, Fixed-term until November 2027 (part-time considered) Faculty: Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology Salary: Level A - $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Level B - $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Level C - $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Work on secure containment for AI agents atop seL4 / collaborate with world-leading formal methods experts / shape next-generation AI-assisted verification tools Apply now to help build safer AI systems and advanced verification methods within a dynamic international research team. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will join an international project securing AI agents using formally verified seL4-based reference monitors. You will develop AI-assisted verification techniques and contribute to large-scale Isabelle/HOL proof engineering. You will also gain experience supervising students and collaborating across academia, government and industry. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Advance research on secure AI containment by designing and verifying seL4-based reference monitors and mechanisms. Develop AI-assisted verification methods and AI proof agents to support large-scale Isabelle/HOL proof maintenance and evolution. Publish high-quality research, present findings at leading conferences, and contribute to collaborative project reports and deliverables. Supervise and mentor students and junior researchers, fostering capability in formal methods and automated reasoning. Collaborate with national and international partners, and contribute to grant proposals, outreach and broader departmental activities. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a PhD in formal methods, automated reasoning, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrate a strong publication record in leading venues, commensurate with experience and opportunity. Bring expertise in areas such as interactive theorem proving, formal verification, security foundations or AI-assisted reasoning. Exhibit excellent analytical, problem-solving and data management skills, with meticulous research record keeping. Communicate complex concepts clearly to technical and non-technical audiences, both in writing and in person. Thrive in collaborative, multi-disciplinary teams and show initiative in leading projects or supervising students. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology You will join the Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology\u2019s vibrant formal methods and security community. Our researchers collaborate across computing, AI and systems to build trustworthy, high-assurance technologies with real-world impact. You will work closely with leading academics and international partners on the Agentic seL4 project. Benefit from strong mentoring, seminar programmes and access to advanced tools, infrastructure and collaborative networks. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. Visa sponsorship is possible; however preference will be given to candidates who can start immediately. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Toby Murray at toby.murray@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Saturday October 24 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Descriptions Word: Postdoctoral Research Fellow (Toby Murray) PD.docx PDF: Postdoctoral Research Fellow (Toby Murray) PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Fellow---Clinical-Diagnostic-Translation-Research_JR-018362": {"title": "Research Fellow \u2013 Clinical Diagnostic Translation Research", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Full Time, Fixed Term for 12 months Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry & Health Sciences Department/ School:\u00a0 Department of Infectious Diseases Salary: Level A - $87,266 - $118,416 or Level B $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Multidisciplinary infectious diseases diagnostics research / real-world clinical and self-collection implementation / national and international field study leadership Join our team to shape future STI diagnostics through impactful translational research and strong clinical partnerships. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will lead clinical diagnostic implementation studies that improve STI detection and care in real-world settings. You will design and deliver multi-site trials, engaging clinicians, laboratories and health services across Australia and internationally. You will gain mentoring within a collaborative team, building a strong track record in translational infectious diseases research. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Lead design and delivery of diagnostic implementation studies, including multi-site STI point-of-care trial expansion and monitoring. Develop study protocols, ethics submissions and regulatory documentation, ensuring compliance and robust governance across all projects. Coordinate participant recruitment, data collection and qualitative and quantitative analysis with clinical and laboratory collaborators. Train and support site staff in research procedures and diagnostic testing, providing remote oversight of study conduct. Mentor students and junior researchers, contribute to manuscripts and presentations, and participate actively in laboratory meetings. \u00a0 You may be a great fit if: \u00a0 Hold a PhD in a relevant discipline or equivalent experience in implementation or infectious diseases research. Bring experience co-designing and delivering research with health services, diagnostic laboratories, clinicians and consumers. Demonstrate expertise implementing medical technologies, ideally diagnostics, within complex multi-stakeholder clinical and laboratory environments. Possess a strong track record in clinical research, including ethics applications, data management and mixed-methods analysis. Excel at managing competing priorities while mentoring junior colleagues and maintaining meticulous project documentation and records. Commit to high scientific and ethical standards and to building effective collaborations with diverse external partners. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 You will join the Pasricha Laboratory within the Department of Infectious Diseases at the Doherty Institute. Our team brings together clinician-scientists, basic scientists, epidemiologists, and public health professionals. We focus on antimicrobial resistance, sexually transmissible infections, HIV, and emerging infections. You will benefit from a collaborative, mentoring-oriented culture that supports impactful translational diagnostics research and career development. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Shivani Pasricha by emailing Shivani.pasricha@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: 8 October. 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 JR-018362 Research Fellow \u2013 Clinical Diagnostic Translation Research PD.pdf JR-018362 Research Fellow \u2013 Clinical Diagnostic Translation Research PD.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Werribee/Clinical-Skills-Support-Nurse_JR-018146": {"title": "Clinical Skills Support Nurse", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed Term until 31/12/2027 Faculty: Science School: Melbourne Veterinary School Salary: UoM 4: $80,400 \u2013 $85,328 p.a. plus 17% super Pivotal role shaping DVM clinical skills / varied multi-species work across leading veterinary sites / supportive expert team Bring your veterinary nursing expertise to empower DVM students and strengthen clinical teaching across Melbourne Veterinary School. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will support DVM students to build essential clinical skills through supervised rotations and practical teaching activities. You will work across leading clinical sites, guiding safe animal handling and nursing practices in multi-species settings. You will join an experienced, collaborative team that values innovation in veterinary education and continuous professional growth. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Provide practical support to DVM students during clinical rotations at Greencross Veterinary Hospital and The Lost Dogs\u2019 Home. Assemble and support clinical skills training, assessments and simulation-based teaching across key veterinary species and procedures. Prepare and maintain anaesthesia, surgical teaching and animal handling resources, ensuring equipment, models and stock meet best-practice standards. Uphold animal care ethics, patient safety and infection-control requirements, supporting veterinarians and students before and after surgery. Build strong relationships with clinical partners, contribute to service improvements, and participate in governance, meetings and training. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a Certificate IV in Veterinary Nursing or equivalent, with proven experience in clinical veterinary practice. Demonstrate confidence in handling cats, dogs and horses, and assisting safely with a range of clinical procedures. Support training and supervision of students or staff, preferably within veterinary clinical skills or teaching environments. Manage clinical spaces, equipment, medicines and consumables, maintaining accurate patient records and documentation to high standards. Apply Occupational Health and Safety practices in clinical settings, prioritising animal welfare, student safety and regulatory compliance. Embrace working across multiple sites and rotations, adapting to changing demands and collaborating within an experienced multidisciplinary team. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 Melbourne Veterinary School The Melbourne Veterinary School, part of the Faculty of Science, is Australia\u2019s number one ranked veterinary school. We deliver Victoria\u2019s only professional entry veterinary medicine program, grounded in strong industry partnerships and impactful clinical education. Within the School, the clinical teaching support team offers over 160 years of combined, multi-species experience. You will join a collaborative, versatile group that values curiosity, sharing ideas and continually improving veterinary teaching and patient care.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Maddi Taper (details in PD) For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: 18 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 JR-018146 Clinical Skills Support Nurse_PD.pdf JR-018146 Clinical Skills Support Nurse_PD.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Casual-Tutors--Faculty-of-Business-and-Economics_JR-018769": {"title": "Casual Tutors, Faculty of Business and Economics", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\n\nTutors are a critical part of teaching teams in the Faculty of Business and Economics. Under the guidance of a Subject Coordinator, Tutors will deliver weekly classes and contribute significantly to students' academic success and satisfaction with university life. The Faculty are seeking Tutors in the departments of Accounting, Economics, Finance, and Management & Marketing. Responsibilities include: - Preparing and delivering high quality, student-centered small group learning experiences - Providing student consultations - Marking assignments and exams About you: You have excellent written and verbal communication skills and a passion for teaching in accounting, economics, finance, management and/or marketing disciplines. You are able to navigate subject websites and teaching technology with ease and work collegially with others. You will also have: - An Honours or postgraduate level qualification in a relevant discipline and/or equivalent relevant academic or professional or practice-based experience and expertise - A current Victorian Working With Children Check (WWCC) by the commencement of the teaching period For further information please refer to the attached PD. Apply today! Applications MUST be submitted via the Casual Tutor Recruitment System (CTRS). Please visit https://ctrs.app.unimelb.edu.au/ctrs/ to apply. Applications submitted using the Apply Now button at the top of this advertisement will NOT \u00a0 be reviewed. Applications Close: Sunday 8th of November at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description Word: 2027 FBE Tutor.docx PDF: 2027 FBE Tutor.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Online-Educator-in-Artificial-Intelligence-or-Cyber-Security_JR-018590": {"title": "Online Educator in Artificial Intelligence or Cyber Security", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nPart time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Part-time (0.4 FTE);\u202fFixed-term\u202ffor 24 months\u00a0 Faculty: Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology Department/ School:\u00a0 School of Computing and Information Systems Salary: Academic Level A $87,266 - $118,416 (pro rata) p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Academic Level B $124,656 \u2013 $148,023 (pro rata) p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 \u202fShape future cyber security or artificial intelligence experts / Flexible remote teaching role / Join top-ranked Computing & Information Systems School\u00a0 \u202fApply now to become a key player in delivering cutting-edge cyber security or artificial intelligence education in a flexible, online environment\u00a0 Investing in you - benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 As an Online Educator in the Master of Cyber Security or Master of Artificial Intelligence programmes, you'll play a crucial role in shaping the next generation of cyber security or artificial intelligence professionals. You'll have the opportunity to facilitate student learning, provide guidance and support, and contribute to the success of this innovative online programme. This flexible, remote role allows you to balance your commitments while making a significant impact in the field of cyber security education or artificial intelligence. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Deliver Exceptional Student Experience: Facilitate engaging learning activities and provide empathetic support to students throughout their online learning journey.\u00a0 Assess and Provide Feedback: Undertake assessment tasks, including marking and providing high-quality, timely feedback to students.\u00a0 Contribute to Teaching Excellence: Prepare and deliver weekly live online workshops, and participate in professional development to enhance teaching practices.\u00a0 Collaborate and Innovate: Work with the academic team to contribute to the improvement of teaching and learning practices in the online cyber security or artificial intelligence programmes.\u00a0 \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a Master's degree or PhD in an IT-related or cognate discipline.\u00a0 Have demonstrated experience in teaching cyber security or artificial intelligence subjects at a university level.\u00a0 Possess a strong understanding of key cyber security or artificial intelligence concepts and disciplines.\u00a0 Have excellent written and verbal communication skills.\u00a0 Be able to work effectively both autonomously and as part of a team.\u00a0 For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 School of Computing and Information Systems The School of Computing & Information Systems (CIS) undertakes research and teaching\u202facross a range of information technology disciplines including Software Engineering, Information Systems, and Computer Science. CIS is the most highly ranked School of Computing and Information Systems in Australia according to all major rankings (THE, QS, ARWU). It offers a comprehensive range of IT and IS courses at all levels, including offerings in science, engineering, and business, and is at the forefront of computing research in Australia and internationally with close links to major computing research initiatives, including Melbourne Bioinformatics, CSL, The Cremorne Digital Hub and CSIRO\u2019s DATA61. The\u202fSchool\u2019s\u202faim is to attract and retain outstanding staff in order to maintain a leading research and teaching profile. We have an existing highly successful research team in the area of the appointment, a large number of PhD students, and a substantial cohort of graduate students in our coursework\u202fMaster\u202fprograms. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Shangqi Lai at shangqi.lai@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 18 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 Word: Online Educator in AI or Cyber Security_PD.docx PDF: Online Educator in AI or Cyber Security_PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Graduate-Research-Examinations-Officer_JR-018404-1": {"title": "Graduate Research Examinations Officer", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nPosition Number: JR-018404 Role type: Full-time; Fixed-term to May 2027\u00a0 Department/School: Student and Scholarly Services \u2013 Graduate Research (Student Administration)\u00a0 Salary: UOM 5: AUD $86,943 - $99,864 p.a. plus 17% super \u00a0 Specialist role in graduate research examinations / collaborate across the University and with external examiners / drive service and process improvement \u00a0 Apply now to play a pivotal role in delivering timely, high-quality outcomes for graduate researchers. \u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. \u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 In this role, you will manage graduate research examinations from submission through to final completion. You will support candidates, academics and examiners while improving processes and reducing delays across the examinations cycle. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Support the end-to-end graduate research examinations process, monitoring progress and intervening early to minimise delays. \u00a0 Interpret and apply policies, advising candidates, examiners and staff on examination requirements, outcomes and available options. \u00a0 Maintain accurate student records, ensuring enrolment, examination and completion data are processed promptly and correctly. \u00a0 Support implementation of viva examinations, system enhancements and process improvements within the Graduate Research team. \u00a0 Contribute positively to team priorities, projects and service reviews to enhance experience for graduate researchers. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a degree-level qualification coupled with relevant administrative or higher education experience. \u00a0 Demonstrate exceptional attention to detail, accuracy and confidence using complex administrative and student systems. \u00a0 Manage competing priorities effectively, organising workload to meet deadlines in a busy, deadline-driven environment. \u00a0 Adapt readily to changing policies, processes and systems, contributing constructively to implementation and continuous improvement. \u00a0 Collaborate proactively within a close-knit team to share knowledge, balance workloads and maximise service efficiency \u00a0 Communicate clearly and diplomatically with diverse stakeholders, maintaining confidentiality in sensitive or complex situations. \u00a0 Bring knowledge of graduate research administration and TechnologyOne, or eagerness to quickly develop this expertise. \u00a0 For further information, please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation \u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 \u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning \u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave \u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 \u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 \u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team You will join the Graduate Research team within Student and Scholarly Services, a collaborative, service-focused group. The team partners with faculties, external examiners and central services to support every stage of graduate research candidature. You will help deliver consistent, high-quality experiences for graduate researchers while contributing to ongoing process and systems improvements . Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin Korin, our Indigenous Employment and Development team, via korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. \u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0 \u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0 \u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. \u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this specific position, contact Mary Makris at m.makris@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email Tien Nguyen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0 Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. \u00a0 Applications close: Sunday, 18th October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: PD_JR-018404 Graduate Research Examinations Officer.docx PD_JR-018404 Graduate Research Examinations Officer.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lecturer-in-Gender-Studies_JR-019042": {"title": "Lecturer in Gender Studies", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Part-time (0.8 FTE); Fixed term until 31 Dec 2029 Faculty: Faculty of Arts Department/ School:\u00a0 School of Social and Political Sciences Salary: Level B - $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Shape cutting-edge Trans and Queer Studies scholarship / inspire diverse Gender Studies students / join a values-driven social sciences school Apply now to lead inclusive Gender Studies teaching and research focused on Trans and Queer Studies scholarship. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will help shape transformative Gender Studies curricula, with a special focus on Trans and Queer Studies scholarship. You will develop your research profile, collaborate across disciplines, and contribute to an inclusive, socially engaged School culture. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Coordinate and deliver engaging undergraduate and honours subjects in Gender Studies, including high-enrolment classes and team teaching. Design inclusive, innovative curricula and technology-enhanced learning resources that support an increasingly diverse student cohort. Undertake high-quality research in Trans and Queer Studies, producing peer-reviewed publications and seeking competitive grant funding. Supervise honours, graduate coursework and research higher degree students, fostering collaborative and multidisciplinary projects. Contribute to School and Faculty leadership, committees, and external engagement, including national and international partnerships. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a completed PhD in Gender Studies or a closely related discipline. Specialise in Trans and Queer Studies with a research focus that complements existing Gender Studies strengths. Demonstrate excellence in tertiary teaching, including coordinating subjects, developing curricula and delivering high-enrolment classes. Supervise honours and postgraduate coursework theses, and contribute to research higher degree supervision. Maintain a strong, developing publication record and show capacity to secure competitive research grants and consultancies. Apply inclusive pedagogies and collegial behaviours that support diverse students and embody Faculty of Arts values. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 School of Social and Political Sciences You will join the School of Social and Political Sciences, a leading hub for innovative social science research. Our multidisciplinary community spans Anthropology, Development Studies, Gender Studies, Criminology, Political Science and Sociology. We are driven by values of intellectual freedom, integrity, social justice, diversity and environmental sustainability. Here, you will collaborate with supportive colleagues to redefine the world and change what matters. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Assoc. Prof. Kalissa Alexeyeff at k.alexeyeff@unimelb.edu. k.alexeyeff@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 1 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 Word: Lecturer in Gender Studies_PD.docx PDF: Lecturer in Gender Studies_PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Thomas-and-Margaret-Ruth-McArthur-Research-Fellow_JR-018964": {"title": "Thomas and Margaret Ruth McArthur Research Fellow", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Full-time; Fixed-term (to 31 December 2029) Faculty: Arts Department/School:\u00a0 School of Social and Political Sciences Salary: Level A $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Prestigious social sciences postdoctoral fellowship / Dedicated research funding up to $10,000 p.a. / Mentoring in a world-class interdisciplinary School Apply now to deepen your research profile and shape debates in anthropology and the social sciences. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 In this prestigious fellowship, you will drive independent research in anthropology or cognate social sciences disciplines. You will strengthen your publication profile, pursue external funding, and collaborate within a vibrant interdisciplinary School. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Lead high-quality independent research in anthropology or cognate social sciences, aligned with School and Faculty priorities. Produce scholarly publications and conference presentations that enhance the School's research reputation. Develop and submit competitive external research grant applications, supported by dedicated fellowship research funds. Contribute to the School's academic life through limited supervision, guest lecturing, and collegial service activities. Maintain safe work practices and comply with OH&S and EH&S responsibilities relevant to your research. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a PhD in Anthropology or a cognate social sciences discipline. Demonstrate capacity for independent scholarship and impactful research in the nominated field. Bring a publication record in refereed journals or publishing houses, relative to opportunity. Communicate clearly and confidently, with strong written and verbal skills for diverse audiences. Collaborate effectively and contribute collegially to an inclusive Faculty of Arts community. Align with Faculty values of diversity, inclusion, and high professional standards. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 School of Social and Political Sciences Within the Faculty of Arts, the School of Social and Political Sciences is a leader in social research. The School brings together Anthropology, Development Studies, Criminology, Gender Studies, Political Science, and Sociology. You will join a collegial community committed to impactful scholarship, public engagement, and distinctive social science education. Senior academics will support your development through mentoring, collaboration, and opportunities to build an enduring research profile. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Associate Professor Jeff Garmany at jeff.garmany@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 1 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: Word: The Thomas and Margaret Ruth McArthur Research Fellow.docx PDF: The Thomas and Margaret Ruth McArthur Research Fellow.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/International-Partnerships-Adviser_JR-019055": {"title": "International Partnerships Adviser", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nole type: Full-time; Fixed term until 31 January 2028\u00a0 Department Chancellery \u2013 Global, Culture and Engagement\u00a0 Salary: UOM 7: $111,275 - $120,453 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0\u00a0 Strategic global partnerships portfolio / Focus on Northeast Asia / Work closely with senior University leaders\u00a0 Apply now to lead strategic international initiatives that strengthen the University\u2019s partnerships, influence and global reach.\u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u202f\u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity: \u00a0 You will support strategic international partnerships, providing analysis, advice and coordination that advance the University\u2019s global ambitions.\u00a0 You will develop relationships across Northeast Asia and beyond, identifying education and research collaboration opportunities and innovative partnership models.\u00a0 What you will deliver: \u00a0 Produce intelligence briefs, market analyses and reports to inform international engagement and partnership decisions in Northeast Asia and other regions.\u00a0 Contribute to business cases, proposals and implementation plans for new institutional partnerships and strategic international projects.\u00a0 Support development and delivery of regional strategies, education and research collaborations, and innovative transnational education initiatives.\u00a0 Coordinate international visits, delegations and events, ensuring effective programming, stakeholder engagement and timely follow-up actions.\u00a0 Maintain high-quality partnership management processes, including relationship tracking, governance support and continuous improvement of systems and frameworks.\u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a relevant degree, or bring an equivalent combination of education, training and international engagement experience.\u00a0 Demonstrate strong written communication skills, including preparing briefs, reports, correspondence and presentations for senior stakeholders.\u00a0 Manage multiple projects and administrative activities concurrently, exercising sound judgement, diplomacy, discretion and careful attention to detail.\u00a0 Build effective relationships with international partners, understanding cross-cultural sensitivities and engaging confidently across diverse contexts.\u00a0 Apply experience in process development and improvement to enhance partnership management, reporting and evaluation practices.\u00a0 Bring experience in higher education, work in another country, or fluency in an Asian language.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u202f\u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u202f\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation\u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u202f\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning\u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u202fand well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.\u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u202f\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u202f\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u202f \u00a0 Your new team: \u00a0 Chancellery Global, Culture and Engagement leads the University\u2019s international strategy, partnerships and global profile.\u00a0 Guided by the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Global, the team shapes regional strategies and advances Advancing Melbourne Globally 2030.\u00a0 You will join talented colleagues who collaborate across faculties and Chancellery, engaging with governments, partners and senior leaders.\u00a0 We offer a dynamic, outward-facing environment where your insights help deliver meaningful international impact for the University.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u202f\u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .\u00a0\u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.\u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0\u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter addressing Essential Selection Criteria 1, 2, 3 found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria \u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this specific position, contact Julie Fam at j.fam@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0\u00a0 Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future.\u00a0 Applications close: \u200b Sunday 18 October 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.\u202f\u00a0 Position Description: \u202f\u00a0 PD_JR-019055 International Partnerships Adviser .pdf PD_JR-019055 International Partnerships Adviser .pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/AURIN-Associate-Software-Engineer_JR-018050": {"title": "AURIN Associate Software Engineer", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term Faculty: Architecture, Building and Planning Department/School: Australian Urban Research Infrastructure Network (AURIN) Salary: AUD $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Entry-level software role in national research infrastructure / build modern cloud, data and API skills / support better urban decisions undefined Join AURIN to grow your engineering career while building platforms that support impactful city and regional research. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 As Associate Software Engineer, you will build and enhance AURIN\u2019s cloud-first platforms for urban research. You will develop technical skills across modern languages, data systems and cloud tools while collaborating with experts. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Contribute to designing, developing, testing and maintaining software components that power AURIN\u2019s digital research infrastructure platforms. Implement integrations and RESTful services that connect data, analytical tools and visualisation systems for researchers and policymakers. Work within a team-based squad, pairing with senior engineers to solve technical challenges and deliver sprint objectives. Apply best-practice version control, CI/CD and containerisation workflows to support reliable, scalable deployments. Participate in documentation, support, and continuous improvement activities, ensuring systems remain usable, secure and well-governed. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold entry-level experience across the software development lifecycle, from requirements gathering through implementation, testing and maintenance. Possess foundational skills in languages such as Java, Python and JavaScript for application development. Understand web servers and databases, including technologies like Apache or Nginx, MongoDB and PostgreSQL. Work effectively within small collaborative teams and balance competing priorities to meet agreed deadlines. Use modern collaborative engineering practices, including git-based workflows, CI/CD, agile delivery and containerised deployments. Demonstrate motivation to up-skill through mentoring, self-directed learning and formal professional development opportunities. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team AURIN sits within the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning, delivering nationally significant urban research infrastructure. You will join a team of engineers, data specialists and researchers enabling evidence-based decisions for cities and regions. The team values collaboration, learning and innovation, providing mentoring, modern tools and opportunities to grow your technical capabilities. By contributing to AURIN\u2019s platforms, you will support research on sustainability, housing, transport and urban resilience across Australia. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Brett Verity at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Position Description: \u00a0 JR-018050 AURIN Associate Software Engineer PD.docx JR-018050 AURIN Associate Software Engineer PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Financial-Aid-Advisor_JR-018524": {"title": "Financial Aid Advisor", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed term for 12 months Department: Student and Scholarly Services Salary: UOM 5 - $86,943 - $99,864 p.a. plus 17% super Meaningful impact on students\u2019 financial wellbeing / shape evolving financial aid and arrival services / thrive in a supportive team Apply now to help students navigate financial assistance options, manage key arrival services, and enhance a vital support offering. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will support students seeking financial assistance and coordinate arrival services for new students.\u00a0 \u00a0 You will process interest-free loans and hardship grants, manage student enquiries, and connect students with wider wellbeing support.\u00a0 \u00a0 You will also contribute to service redesign and continuous improvement in a collaborative and supportive team. What you will deliver: Assess student loan and hardship grant applications, ensuring accurate eligibility decisions and timely processing of payments. Engage directly with students via appointments and enquiries, triaging cases, escalating appropriately, and referring to specialist services. Oversee day-to-day delivery of arrival services, liaising with external contractors and stakeholders to ensure smooth operations. Maintain accurate service information and templates across channels, including Teams, OneCRM and student-facing web content. Contribute to service improvement initiatives, supporting digital uplifts, process redesigns, and reviews that enhance student experience. \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: Hold a tertiary qualification in a relevant discipline, or equivalent education and experience in student services. Demonstrate experience in university student services or lending environments, working with financial hardship and wellbeing contexts. Manage time-critical workloads and competing demands with strong organisational skills and attention to detail. Operate confidently within protocols, policies and compliance requirements, applying privacy and confidentiality in record-keeping. Use CRM systems such as Salesforce, plus Microsoft Teams, Word, Excel and Outlook in daily work. Contribute proactively to a small, collaborative team, adapting to service redesign and continuous improvement activities. \u00a0 For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team \u2013 Student and Scholarly Services Student and Scholarly Services sits within Chancellery and provides end-to-end administration, wellbeing and scholarly support. Within this area, the Financial Aid team supports students through loans, hardship grants and coordinated arrival services. You will join a lean, positive team that values collaboration, flexibility, innovation and enhancing the student experience. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at oied-hr@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. \u00a0 Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Alex Spezza at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday the 18th of October 2026 at 11:55 PM; AEDT. Position Description: JR-018524 - Financial Aid Advisor - PD.pdf JR-018524 - Financial Aid Advisor - PD.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Lecturer--Nursing_JR-018635": {"title": "Lecturer, Nursing", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u00a00.5-1FTE; Fixed term for 12 Months Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/School: Department of Nursing; Melbourne School of Health Sciences Salary: $124,656 - $148,023 (pro-rata for part time) plus 17% super\u00a0 Lead entry-to-practice and postgraduate mental health nursing education / shape evidence-based curriculum and simulation / partner with leading clinical services Contribute to the Department\u2019s commitment to fostering excellent mental health nursing education that advances practice, supports students and deepens impactful clinical partnerships. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will coordinate and teach mental health nursing across postgraduate programs, shaping high-quality, evidence-based learning experiences. You will connect classroom, simulation and clinical practice, working closely with students and expert clinical partners. You will also contribute to scholarship in learning and teaching within a supportive, research-informed academic community. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Coordinate and teach mental health nursing subjects across entry-to-practice and postgraduate programs using contemporary blended and online approaches. Develop, review and enhance curriculum and simulation activities grounded in evidence-based mental health nursing practice. Translate research and scholarship into innovative teaching that improves student learning and professional readiness. Build and maintain strong relationships with clinical partners and contribute expertise to professional and community activities. Engage actively in departmental and school committees, supporting inter-professional learning and strategic educational initiatives. \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold current AHPRA registration as a Registered Nurse and a master\u2019s degree in nursing. Possess postgraduate qualifications and recent clinical experience in mental health nursing practice. Demonstrate advanced therapeutic skills, including expertise in child safety standards and family violence responses. Bring experience delivering mental health nursing education and coordinating programs or subjects within nursing. Maintain an established scholarship profile in learning and teaching, including publications or conference presentations. Communicate and collaborate effectively with students, colleagues and external stakeholders to support high-quality education. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team You will join the Department of Nursing within the Melbourne School of Health Sciences at the Parkville campus. The team is committed to excellence in nursing education, research and clinical partnership, with strong connections to leading health services. Colleagues value collaboration, compassion, respect, integrity and accountability, and champion diversity and inclusion. You will work alongside experienced educators and researchers who are dedicated to improving mental health care and enhancing student success. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and relevant experience and addressing the Key Selection Criteria (KSC) outlined in the Position Description in one document . Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Fiona Newell at fnewall@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Please note - Applications will be reviewed as they are received. Applications close: Monday 19th October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 JR-018635 Lecturer, Nursing - PD.docx JR-018635 Lecturer, Nursing - PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Curator--Rare-Books_JR-018643-1": {"title": "Curator, Rare Books", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nPosition Number: JR-018643 Role type: Full-time; Continuing Location: Parkville Department: Archives and Special Collections (Student and Scholarly Services) Salary: UOM 7: AUD $111,275 - $120,453 p.a. plus 17% super Steward an internationally significant rare books collection and join the team leading \u00a0creative public engagement initiatives Apply now to curate a collection that powers research, teaching innovation and inspires public engagement at Melbourne. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will curate and interpret the University\u2019s Rare Books Collection for diverse audiences. You will help drive object-based learning, research and exhibitions, while enhancing access through contributing to cataloguing, digitisation and outreach. You will shape how this extraordinary collection supports innovative scholarship across disciplines. What you will deliver: Provide oversight for physical access, storage, handling, movement and loans, ensuring best practice care of rare materials. Develop exhibitions and interpretive programs, underpinned by collection-based research, to broaden discovery and engagement. Design and deliver object-based learning and collection literacy programs embedded within curriculum and e-learning environments. Contribute to digitisation, catalogue enrichment and rights management to improve discovery and responsible access to materials. Advise on acquisitions, donations and deaccessions, ensuring compliant trust fund use and robust collection documentation. \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: Hold postgraduate qualifications in Museum or Curatorial Studies, or equivalent experience with historical or cultural collections. Possess tertiary qualifications in humanities or Library Science, or an equivalent blend of education and experience. Bring deep knowledge of rare and antiquarian books, manuscripts and print history, including collecting and publishing contexts. Demonstrate experience curating exhibitions and access programs, and interpreting complex material for diverse and non-specialist audiences. Apply advanced skills in cataloguing, condition reporting, collection management systems and safe handling of cultural heritage materials. Collaborate confidently with academics, donors, booksellers and professional staff within large, multifaceted institutions or libraries. Embrace emerging digital, metadata and learning technologies to enhance collection discovery, teaching integration and online engagement. For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team \u00a0 You will join Archives and Special Collections, a specialist department within Student and Scholarly Services. The team develops, cares for and shares outstanding archives and special collections for teaching, learning and research. You will work with collegial experts who value collaboration, innovation, service excellence and inclusive, community-focused engagement. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. \u00a0 Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Jon Buckingham via jon.buckingham@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Tien Nguyen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Monday 26th October at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: PD_JR-018643 Curator, Rare Books.docx PD_JR-018643 Curator, Rare Books.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/STEM-Outreach-Programs-Coordinator--STEM-EDGE_JR-018864": {"title": "STEM Outreach Programs Coordinator, STEM EDGE", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-Term for 12 months \u00a0 Faculty: Faculty of Science\u00a0 Department/ School: Student and Academic Services \u00a0 Salary: UoM 6: $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Champion equity-focused STEM outreach for girls and gender diverse students / coordinate flagship programs across Science / grow your impact in a supportive, inclusive faculty.\u00a0 Apply now to share your STEM passion, equity values and personal story while shaping students' futures.\u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity \u00a0 In this identified role, you will coordinate STEM EDGE and contribute to programs that inspire young people to explore opportunities in STEM.\u00a0 You will coordinate engaging camps, events and mentoring experiences that build confidence, develop skills and connect students with STEM role models.\u00a0 You\u2019ll be part of a values-driven team committed to equity and improving the representation of women and gender-diverse people in science.\u00a0 As part of the Faculty\u2019s commitment to improving representation in science, this role is open only to applicants who are women or gender diverse, in line with the special measure provided for under section 12 of the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic). \u00a0 What you will deliver: \u00a0\u00a0 Plan, develop and coordinate STEM EDGE, The Science Experience and other outreach programs within the Faculty.\u00a0 Promote programs, recruit participants and schools, and manage logistics to ensure safe, engaging and inclusive experiences.\u00a0 Build and maintain relationships with students, families, schools, mentors and University staff to support participation and impact.\u00a0 Recruit, train and support student mentors and volunteers, fostering community and positive role modelling for participants.\u00a0 Monitor program outcomes, evaluate and report data, and identify risks and improvements, including child safety requirements\u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0\u00a0 Hold a relevant tertiary qualification or equivalent combination of education, training and related experience.\u00a0 Demonstrate passion for STEM education and enthusiasm for engaging school students, especially girls and gender diverse students.\u00a0 Bring project management skills to deliver programs on time, within budget and with evidenced positive outcomes.\u00a0 Possess experience working with schools or youth programs and understanding of duty-of-care and child safety responsibilities.\u00a0 Show awareness of equity issues and barriers facing girls and gender diverse students pursuing STEM pathways.\u00a0 Demonstrate confidence communicating with varied stakeholders and initiative to host events, workshops and student activities.\u00a0 For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation\u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning\u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave\u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page! \u00a0 Your new team \u2013 Student and Academic Services \u00a0 You will join the Faculty of Science\u2019s Student and Academic Services team, dedicated to transformative STEM education. The team leads flagship outreach programs that connect diverse school communities with inspiring scientists and authentic university experiences.\u00a0\u00a0 We champion equity, inclusion and belonging, particularly for girls and gender diverse students exploring STEM futures. You will collaborate across schools, institutes and partners, gaining broad networks and support to grow your outreach expertise.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .\u00a0\u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.\u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria \u00a0 \u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this specific position, contact Marie Kinsey at marie.kinsey@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Apply today and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future.\u00a0 Applications close: 25 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0\u00a0 JR-018864 - STEM Outreach Programs Coordinator, STEM EDGE - PD.docx \u00a0 JR-018864 - STEM Outreach Programs Coordinator, STEM EDGE - PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Software-Engineer_JR-018676": {"title": "Research Software Engineer", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term (2 years) Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/School: Melbourne School of Population and Global Health, Centre for Epidemiology and Biostatistics Salary: UOM 6 - $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. plus 17% superannuation Shape epidemic forecasting infrastructure across Australia and New Zealand / focus on professional software engineering, not academic publishing / collaborate with leading epidemiologists, statisticians and data scientists Apply now to use your software engineering expertise to deliver robust epidemic analytics tools that inform policy Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity You will design and deliver open-source software that powers national epidemic forecasting and scenario modelling hubs. You will work closely with leading epidemiologists and data scientists, turning complex health data into actionable insights. This professional software engineering role lets you focus on building high-quality tools rather than academic publications. What you will deliver: Plan, design, develop, test and deploy open-source research software that supports ACEFA\u2019s forecasting and scenario modelling hubs. Create modern web interfaces, dashboards and user experiences, integrating secure back-end services, databases and cloud infrastructure. Engage software users and collaborators through code repositories and forums, supporting multi-team participation in epidemic analytics. Implement robust data management, security and confidentiality practices aligned with population health research policies and standards. Produce clear technical documentation, reports and project communications that enable reuse, scalability and ongoing maintenance of tools. \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: Hold a bachelor\u2019s degree in computer science, software engineering or a similarly computational discipline. Design and implement high-quality research software using R, Python, JavaScript or comparable data-focused programming languages. Develop modern web interfaces and visualisations using JavaScript frameworks, HTML5, CSS3 and relevant libraries. Apply strong software engineering practices, including Git, testing, continuous integration and high-performance computing skills. Manage project workload effectively, meeting milestones and deadlines while maintaining clean, reproducible and well-documented code. Collaborate within multidisciplinary teams, communicating complex technical concepts clearly to non-technical public health stakeholders. \u00a0 For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! \u00a0 Your new team \u2013 Infectious Disease Dynamics Unit You will join the Infectious Disease Dynamics Unit within the Centre for Epidemiology and Biostatistics. Our team collaborates across universities, government and ARDC partners to tackle pressing infectious disease threats. You will work in a values-driven, multidisciplinary environment that blends data science, public health and impactful research. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Alex Spezza at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 18th of October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: JR-018676 - Research Software Engineer - PD.docx JR-018676 - Research Software Engineer - PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Shepparton-Victoria/Deputy-Director--Medical-Student-Education--Echuca_JR-018936-1": {"title": "Deputy Director, Medical Student Education- Echuca", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nPart time\n\nRole type: \u00a02x 0.25 FTE positions; Continuing Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department:\u00a0 Department of Rural Health Salary: Level C: $152,695 - $176,065 per annum (pro rata for part-time) p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Shape rural medical education for MD students / build partnerships with regional health services / lead a supportive student culture Join us to lead clinical teaching in regional Victoria and support the next generation of rural doctors. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will lead and deliver high quality clinical education for MD students based at our Echuca campus. You will shape rural medical training, support student wellbeing, and work with clinicians across the Rural Clinical School. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Lead implementation and continual improvement of the clinical curriculum for MD students within the Rural Clinical School. Collaborate with Echuca Regional Health and Deputy Directors at other sites to coordinate teaching and student rotations. Provide regular academic and pastoral support, including weekly meetings, to ensure students thrive in regional placements. Deliver high quality clinical teaching, assessment, and course coordination aligned with University policies and standards. Engage in research and scholarly activity, including supervising MD research students and contributing to rural health projects. \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a medical qualification recognised in Australia, plus college fellowship or equivalent clinically relevant postgraduate degree. Bring extensive experience in clinical teaching, medical education, and curriculum delivery for medical students. Maintain active clinical practice in the Wangaratta region and demonstrate national recognition within your clinical specialty. Demonstrate strong course coordination, organisational, and administrative skills within a university or teaching hospital environment. Build collaborative relationships with students, clinicians, and health services, providing high quality academic and pastoral support. Show familiarity with Victorian regional hospitals and an interest in rural health and rural medical education. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 The Department of Rural Health sits within the Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences and delivers rural clinical education across multiple regional sites, including Echuca, Ballarat, Shepparton and Wangaratta. The team works closely with partner health services to provide high quality medical training that strengthens rural workforces and improves community health outcomes. You will join a supportive, values-driven culture that champions collaboration, compassion, respect, integrity and accountability, and invests in staff development and wellbeing. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Steven Bismire For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: 23 October. 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 JR-018936 Deputy Director, Medical Student Education- Echuca PD.docx JR-018936 Deputy Director, Medical Student Education- Echuca PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lawyer--MDHS-_JR-018932-1": {"title": "Lawyer (MDHS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing\u00a0 Department: Legal and Risk, COO Portfolio\u00a0 Salary: UOM 8 - $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Advise on complex health, research and commercial law matters / partner with Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences (MDHS) and Melbourne Research and Enterprise (MRE) leaders / grow versatile in-house legal expertise\u00a0 Apply now to shape strategic legal outcomes for health, research and education across a leading university.\u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u202f\u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity: \u00a0 You will provide high-quality legal advice on complex health, research and commercial matters across MDHS and MRE. You will work as a trusted partner to senior stakeholders, shaping strategic decisions and managing legal risk.\u00a0 What you will deliver: \u00a0 Provide clear, solution-focused legal advice on areas including research, clinical trials, IP, employment, regulation and student placements.\u00a0 Draft, review and negotiate diverse commercial agreements, including research, technology, licensing and collaboration arrangements.\u00a0 Build strong partnerships with MDHS and MRE stakeholders, enabling early legal input into key initiatives.\u00a0 Support Legal and Risk projects, driving process improvements and consistent, efficient legal service delivery.\u00a0 Manage routine and complex matters independently while collaborating closely with colleagues across the MDHS cluster and the Legal and Risk team.\u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a law degree, at least two years\u2019 PQE and a current Victorian Practising Certificate.\u00a0 Bring experience in commercial contracts and one or more of research, clinical trials, IP or digital health.\u00a0 Demonstrate strong drafting, analytical and negotiation skills, with a focus on practical, solution-oriented advice.\u00a0 Manage competing priorities and ambiguity while exercising sound judgment about when to escalate or seek guidance.\u00a0 Build productive relationships with diverse stakeholders and contribute positively to a collaborative, high-performing team.\u00a0 Maintain high ethical standards, professionalism and confidentiality when dealing with sensitive or complex legal matters.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u202f\u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u202f\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation\u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u202f\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as\u00a0 learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning\u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u202fand well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave\u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u202f\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u202f\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u202f \u00a0 Your new team: \u00a0 Legal Services is the University\u2019s in-house legal team providing strategic legal support across teaching, research and operations. You will join the MDHS cluster, working closely with leaders in medicine, dentistry, health sciences and research. The team champions Service Excellence, One Team and University First, fostering collaboration, learning and continuous improvement.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u202f\u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.\u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0\u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0\u00a0 Please use the cover letter and resume template (under Application Document) below to create and outline your experience. Resumes not in the template below will not be accepted.\u00a0 \u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this specific position, contact Alex Maschmedt at\u00a0 amaschmedt@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email Ethan Khamly at ethan.khamly@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0\u00a0 Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future.\u00a0 Applications Close: \u200b Sunday 25th October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u202f\u00a0 Application Document: Template CV - UOM8 Lawyer (MDHS)- 2026.docx \u00a0 Position Description: \u202f JR-018932 Lawyer (MDHS) PD.pdf \u00a0 JR-018932 Lawyer (MDHS) PD.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Senior-Lecturer-Social-Change-and-Education-Policy_JR-019132": {"title": "Senior Lecturer Social Change and Education Policy", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Full time, fixed term (four years) Faculty: Education Department/School:\u00a0 Education and Society Salary: Level C - $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Shape education systems through critical policy scholarship / drive social change with impactful research / teach diverse future educators Join a collaborative academic community to advance education policy scholarship and inspire students committed to social justice Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will lead innovative teaching and research on social change, education policy and governance. You will design and coordinate subjects across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, including online and blended delivery. You will collaborate with colleagues and partners to influence educational futures locally, nationally and internationally. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Design, deliver and coordinate engaging subjects on social change and education policy across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. Embed Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander education and diverse social justice perspectives throughout curricula and teaching practice. Build a high-quality research programme, securing competitive funding and publishing in leading international outlets. Supervise Graduate Research students and contribute to research projects focused on education policy and social change. Contribute academic leadership and service through committees, professional associations and collaborative partnerships with external stakeholders. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a PhD or research doctorate in education or a closely related discipline. Demonstrate strong expertise in education policy, governance and social change, including contemporary theoretical and policy debates. Show a developing track record of high-quality publications and success in securing competitive research funding. Bring experience designing, coordinating and delivering tertiary subjects, with confidence using digital and blended pedagogies. Provide evidence of effective Graduate Research supervision or co-supervision within relevant fields. Engage collaboratively across disciplines and with external partners, including communities, governments and professional associations. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 Faculty of Education Within the Faculty of Education, the School of Education and Society brings together scholars committed to understanding education as a public, relational and ethical endeavour. The team leads impactful research and teaching that informs policy, professional practice and system reform, with a strong focus on equity, inclusion and sustainability. You will join a collegial community that values innovation, partnership and research-led teaching to shape more just educational futures. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Marcia McKenzie at marcia.mckenzie@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 25 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description - Word: JR-019132_Senior Lecturer, Social Change and Education Policy_PD.docx PDF: JR-019132_Senior Lecturer, Social Change and Education Policy_PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lecturer-Health-and-Physical-Education_JR-019063": {"title": "Lecturer Health and Physical Education", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Full time; Fixed term to 30 November 2027 Faculty: Education Department/School:\u00a0 School of Education and Society Salary: Level B - $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Advance excellence in teaching, research, and leadership within the Health, Outdoor, Physical Education and Sport disciplines, contributing meaningfully to both the Faculty of Education and broader academic community.\u00a0 Engage with innovative pedagogies and interdisciplinary scholarship, and shape the future of education in a dynamic and supportive environment.\u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will be a member of an academic team within the Health, Outdoor, Physical Education and Sport (HOPES) specialisation in the Faculty of Education, and play a pivotal role in advancing teaching, scholarship, engagement, and leadership while also contributing more broadly to the fields of teaching and teacher education. You will coordinate and deliver subjects at both undergraduate and graduate levels, with a particular emphasis on the Faculty of Education\u2019s undergraduate breadth subjects and graduate initial teacher education programmes. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Preparing and delivering diverse teaching modalities, developing and reviewing curricula, and coordinating subject administration including supervision of sessional staff.\u00a0 Assessing student progress through timely feedback, marking, moderation, supporting practical application of theory, and monitoring teaching quality for continuous improvement.\u00a0 Conducting and disseminating scholarship in the Health, Outdoor, Physical Education and Sport (HOPES) field, securing research funding, supervising graduate research students, and engaging in national and international academic forums.\u00a0 Providing leadership through contributing to professional development, participating in faculty and university committees, supporting colleagues, and engaging with relevant professional and community organisations.\u00a0 \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a PhD or research doctorate relevant to the Health, Outdoor, Physical Education and Sport (HOPES) specialist area with a focus on education and teaching.\u00a0 Demonstrate experience in course or subject development, subject coordination, and providing specialised support to students on placement.\u00a0 Maintain a developing research profile with a strong record of publications in leading academic outlets and the ability to contribute to research funding and supervision of graduate research students.\u00a0 Possess proficiency in digital literacy and familiarity with contemporary digital pedagogies to support innovative research, learning, and teaching practices.\u00a0 For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team \u2013 Faculty of Education The Faculty of Education (FoE) fosters staff productivity, growth and engagement in a collective effort to enrich the contribution that education makes to society. We conduct research and teaching that leads to the transformation of education practice both within and beyond the profession. Faculty of Education stimulates learning that enriches the potential of students from around the world, enabling meaningful careers and profound contributions to communities. We provide research leadership, setting the direction for high-impact, innovative and responsive research that addresses the pressing issues of our time. We lead purposeful engagement with society, sharing our resources and expertise as part of collaborative efforts to build a resilient, equitable and sustainable future.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact John Quay at jquay@unimelb.edu.au . For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: JR-019063_Lecturer, Health and Physical Education_PD.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lecturer-in-Buddhist-Studies-and-Philosophy_JR-018900-1": {"title": "Lecturer in Buddhist Studies and Philosophy", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Part-time (0.8 FTE); Fixed-term (3 years); Level B Education-focused Faculty: Arts School:\u00a0 Historical and Philosophical Studies Salary: Level B: AUD $124,656 \u2013 $148,023 p.a. (pro rata) plus 17% super Shape an innovative Buddhist Studies minor / champion inclusive, student-centred teaching / contribute to a leading humanities faculty Apply now to advance Buddhist Studies and Philosophy education while building your research and engagement profile. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will lead engaging, inclusive teaching in Buddhist Studies and Philosophy across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. You will also build your research profile and partnerships, contributing to SHAPS\u2019 vibrant intellectual and community life. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Design, coordinate and deliver high-quality subjects for the Buddhist Studies minor and related Philosophy offerings. Innovate curricula, including technology-enhanced learning and cross-disciplinary, student mobility or overseas teaching experiences. Produce high-quality research outputs and actively contribute to seminars, conferences and broader disciplinary networks. Foster inclusive, respectful classrooms that support diverse learners and embed Buddhist philosophical perspectives. Contribute to School and Faculty service through committees, compliance, and ongoing professional development. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a completed PhD in Philosophy, History, Religious Studies or a cognate discipline focused on Buddhist Studies. Demonstrate a developing track record of high-quality research publications or equivalent scholarly outputs. Deliver excellent, inclusive teaching and lead subject or course development within Buddhist Studies and Philosophy. Coordinate subjects or programmes, including supervising tutors and supporting honours or postgraduate coursework students. Use educational technologies confidently to design engaging learning, assessment and online or blended experiences. Build respectful collegial relationships and external networks, including community or industry engagement where possible. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team SHAPS brings together History, Philosophy, Classics, Archaeology and related disciplines in a vibrant humanities community. You will join colleagues committed to rigorous scholarship, inclusive teaching and rich engagement with local and global partners. As part of the Faculty of Arts, you will help educate critical, ethically minded graduates. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Hannah Gould at hannah.gould@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Petra at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 JR-018900 - Lecturer in Buddhist Studies and Philosophy_PD.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Senior-Lecturer--Economics---Econometrics_JR-018940-1": {"title": "Senior Lecturer, Economics / Econometrics", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Full time, Continuing Faculty: Faculty of Business and Economics Department/ School:\u00a0 Department of Economics Salary: Level C - $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 World-leading economics and econometrics department / Shape research, teaching and policy impact / Cooperative, well-resourced environment Join a top-ranked department to lead influential research, inspire students, and mentor future economists and econometricians. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. We strongly encourage applications from women and people who identify as women The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will lead high-quality research in economics or econometrics within a globally recognised, impact-driven department. You will teach engaged students, supervise ambitious researchers, and help shape innovative curricula and academic leadership. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Produce internationally recognised research and actively secure external funding through grants, fellowships and collaborations. Deliver high-quality teaching in specialist and core subjects across undergraduate, honours and graduate programmes. Supervise and mentor honours, masters and PhD students, fostering rigorous research training and academic development. Provide academic leadership through subject coordination, curriculum innovation and contributions to departmental strategy and governance. Engage with government, industry and community partners to translate research insights into policy and practice. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a PhD in economics, econometrics or a closely related discipline, completed by commencement. Demonstrate a strong, sustained research record with publications in leading peer-reviewed economics or econometrics journals. Teach effectively in specialist economics or econometrics subjects and contribute to core undergraduate or postgraduate teaching. Secure, or show strong potential to secure, competitive research funding and cooperative policy or industry projects. Provide high-quality supervision and mentoring for honours, masters and PhD students across diverse research topics. Contribute positively to departmental leadership, service, curriculum development and external engagement with government or industry. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 Department of Economics You will join the Faculty of Business and Economics, renowned for teaching with an impact, research and industry engagement. Within the Department of Economics, you will collaborate with leading economists, econometricians and actuaries across diverse fields. You will engage with specialised research units and labs, from applied microeconomics to market design and environmental data. Our collaborative, inclusive culture supports excellence, mentoring and strong connections with business, government and the wider community. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Phillip McCalman at econ-jobhire@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: 15 January 2027 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 Word: JR-018940_Senior Lecturer in Economics and Econometrics_PD.docx PDF: \u00a0 JR-018940_Senior Lecturer in Economics and Econometrics_PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Fellow-in-Nanocrystal-Synthesis_JR-018907": {"title": "Research Fellow in Nanocrystal Synthesis", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-Term for 12 months\u00a0 Faculty: Faculty of Science\u00a0 Department/ School: School of Chemistry\u00a0 Salary: Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super (PhD entry level $110,319)\u00a0 \u00a0 Drive nanocrystal innovation for ultra-low-cost solar PV / Collaborate across chemistry and engineering / Build your independent research profile.\u00a0 Apply now to advance innovative nanocrystal synthesis for ultra-low-cost solar technologies within a supportive, diverse team.\u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will lead the synthesis and scale-up of nanocrystals that enable ultra-low-cost solar technologies.\u00a0 You will collaborate with leading chemists and engineers while developing your independent research profile and leadership skills.\u00a0 You will also mentor students and contribute to a safe, inclusive and high-performing laboratory environment.\u00a0 What you will deliver: \u00a0\u00a0 Conduct innovative research on nanocrystal synthesis, scale-up and characterisation aligned with ACAP solar PV objectives.\u00a0 Lead daily operations, safety, and maintenance of the materials synthesis laboratory and specialist equipment.\u00a0 Supervise and mentor undergraduate and postgraduate researchers, contributing to training, risk assessments and group meetings.\u00a0 Collaborate with national ACAP partners, preparing reports, publications and presentations on research progress and outcomes.\u00a0 Develop new project ideas and competitive funding applications to build your independent research trajectory.\u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0\u00a0 Hold a PhD in physical chemistry or materials science and engineering, or an equivalent discipline.\u00a0 Bring demonstrated experience in nanomaterials synthesis and characterisation, including nanocrystal purification and scale-up.\u00a0 Demonstrate a strong publication record and ability to write high-quality reports and journal manuscripts.\u00a0 Manage laboratory-based projects safely, including equipment upkeep, OH&S compliance and risk assessment approval processes.\u00a0 Collaborate effectively in multidisciplinary teams and engage with industry or professional stakeholders on joint projects.\u00a0 Show potential to develop independent research directions, secure funding and supervise or mentor student researchers.\u00a0 For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation\u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning\u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave\u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page! \u00a0 Your new team \u2013 School of Chemistry \u00a0 Based in the School of Chemistry within the Faculty of Science, you will join a collegial community. You will work closely with the Australian Centre for Advanced Photovoltaics node and interdisciplinary engineering collaborators. Our shared focus is advancing renewable energy technologies while fostering diversity, inclusion, professional development and research excellence.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .\u00a0\u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.\u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria \u00a0 \u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this specific position, contact Associate Professor Wallace Wong at wwhwong@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Apply today and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future.\u00a0 Applications close: 29 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0\u00a0 JR-018907 - Research Fellow in Nanocrystal Synthesis - PD.docx JR-018907 - Research Fellow in Nanocrystal Synthesis - PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Security-Leader_JR-018634-1": {"title": "Research Security Leader", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing\u00a0 Department: MRE \u2013 Office of Research Ethics and Integrity\u00a0 Salary: UOM 9 - $146,049 - $151,951 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Strategic research security leadership role / shape compliant global collaborations and protect University reputation / partner with senior academics, regulators and government\u00a0 Apply now to lead a high-performing research security team and influence secure, responsible research across the University.\u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u202f\u00a0 \u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u202f\u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity: \u00a0 You will lead a specialist research security team supporting secure, compliant and impactful research collaborations.\u00a0 You will advise on complex national security, export controls and foreign interference risks across a leading research-intensive university.\u00a0 You will shape education, guidance and policy that strengthen research integrity and protect the University\u2019s global reputation.\u00a0 What you will deliver: \u00a0 Lead and mentor Research Security Advisors to deliver high-quality, timely advice and support to researchers.\u00a0 Oversee allocation, workflow and prioritisation across the team, balancing reactive requests and proactive compliance initiatives.\u00a0 Provide expert guidance on complex research security matters, including export controls, sanctions and foreign interference obligations.\u00a0 Monitor emerging geopolitical, research security and cybersecurity risks, and translate insights into strengthened protections and practices.\u00a0 Develop and deliver training, briefings and communications, and represent the University in engagements with regulators and sector forums.\u00a0 \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold postgraduate qualifications in a relevant discipline, or equivalent experience in research, security or compliance.\u00a0 Demonstrate deep knowledge of research security, foreign interference guidance and related legislation affecting the university sector.\u00a0 Apply advanced analytical thinking and sound judgement to complex, sensitive and ethical research risk scenarios.\u00a0 Communicate complex regulatory and security concepts clearly through training, briefings, outreach and written guidance.\u00a0 Lead and develop staff, drive efficient team operations and build strong relationships across diverse stakeholder groups.\u00a0 Draft regulatory submissions, engage confidently with government and regulators, and remain curious about emerging research security issues.\u00a0 \u00a0 What we offer you! \u202f\u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u202f\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation\u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u202f\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning\u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u202fand well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave\u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u202f\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u202f\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u202f \u00a0 \u00a0 Your new team: \u00a0 Melbourne Research & Enterprise supports researchers to secure funding, translate discoveries and deliver real-world impact. Within this division, the Office of Research Ethics and Integrity leads governance, ethics, integrity and research security frameworks. You will join a collaborative, service-oriented team that partners with academics, professional staff, institutes and government. The team values integrity, responsiveness and continuous improvement, offering rich exposure to complex national security issues and sector-leading practice. \u00a0 Be Yourself \u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u202f\u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .\u202f\u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.\u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0\u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this specific position, contact Mel Green\u00a0 at mel.green@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at Alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future.\u00a0 Applications close: \u200b Wednesday 21 October 2026 -11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.\u202f\u00a0 Position Description: \u202f\u00a0 PD_JR-018634 Research Security Leader .pdf PD_JR-018634 Research Security Leader .docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Facilities-Specialist-Lead--Peter-Doherty-Institute_JR-017797-1": {"title": "Facilities Specialist Lead, Peter Doherty Institute", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing\u00a0 Department: Campus Operations & Delivery, Enterprise Services Group\u00a0 Salary: UOM 8 - AUD $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Influential facilities management leadership in world-class infectious disease institute / safeguard complex containment facilities / expanding remit including AIID\u00a0 Apply now to lead critical facilities that enable cutting-edge infectious disease research and care.\u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u202f\u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity: \u00a0 In this role, you will lead the Doherty Engineering Group supporting a world-class infectious diseases institute.\u00a0 You will oversee complex building services, safeguard high-containment laboratories and shape systems for the future AIID facility.\u00a0 What you will deliver: \u00a0 Lead and develop the Doherty Engineering Group, fostering collaboration and high-quality support for diverse bio-research professionals.\u00a0 Coordinate facilities management, maintenance and engineering activities to support Joint Venture objectives and Institute teaching and research.\u00a0 Identify and mitigate infrastructure risks, ensuring safety, compliance, business continuity and accurate engineering documentation.\u00a0 Manage asset monitoring systems, long-term replacement planning and annual operational budgets for building services and equipment.\u00a0 Oversee consultants, contractors and lease obligations, representing COD in key committees and driving continuous operational improvement.\u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a degree in engineering or equivalent experience and technical aptitude in facilities management within in a complex building environment.\u00a0 Lead and contribute to an operationally focussed facilities teams within large, technically complex buildings, ideally health, hospital or laboratory settings.\u00a0 Apply strong project and contractor management skills, including risk assessment and root cause analysis.\u00a0 Demonstrate broad technical aptitude across mechanical, electrical, hydraulic and laboratory engineering services and control systems.\u00a0 Use digital control systems and information management tools confidently to monitor, document and improve building performance.\u00a0 Have excellent interpersonal and communication skills with a demonstrated ability to engage with a range of building stakeholders.\u00a0 Manage operational budgets and financial reporting, including building outgoings and cost-saving opportunities.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u202f\u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u202f\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation\u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u202f\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning\u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u202fand well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave\u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u202f\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u202f\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u202f \u00a0 Your new team: \u00a0 Campus Operations & Delivery sits within the Enterprise Services Group, supporting over 600 buildings across the University.\u00a0 At the Peter Doherty Institute, you will join a specialised facilities team enabling infectious disease research and care.\u00a0 You will collaborate with University and health partners, shaping high-containment infrastructure and future operations for the AIID facility.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u202f\u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.\u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0\u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.\u00a0\u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this specific position, contact Brian Cullen at cullen.b@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0\u00a0 Apply today and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future.\u00a0 Applications close: \u200bWednesday 21 October 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.\u202f\u00a0 Position Description: \u202f PD_JR-017797 Facilities Specialist Lead \u2013 Peter Doherty Institute.docx\u00a0 PD_JR-017797 Facilities Specialist Lead \u2013 Peter Doherty Institute.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Operations-Manager--Melbourne-National-Security-and-Defence_JR-019056": {"title": "Operations Manager, Melbourne National Security and Defence", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term (2 years)\u00a0 Department: Chancellery \u2013 Melbourne Research and Enterprise\u00a0 Salary: UOM 10 - $156,480 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Institution-wide leadership on national security and defence / shape new governance and operating frameworks / high-visibility senior role\u00a0 Join us to build a resilient, secure University and lead operational excellence across national security initiatives.\u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u202f\u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity: \u00a0 In this senior role, you will establish and lead the operating framework for Melbourne National Security and Defence (MNSD). Reporting to the Associate Director, Research Collaboration, you will work closely with the MNSD Executive Director and specialist University teams, to support effective governance, risk management and compliance for complex, sensitive activities. You will assist coordinated engagement with government, industry and research partners to strengthen the University's defence capabilities.\u00a0\u00a0 What you will deliver: \u00a0 Provide strategic operational leadership for MNSD, developing and implementing its architecture, operating framework and core activities.\u00a0 Oversee day-to-day operations, workflows and resourcing across national security and defence initiatives, aligning activity with University priorities.\u00a0 Lead governance, risk and compliance processes, ensuring robust controls and adherence to DISP and institutional frameworks.\u00a0 Build strong relationships with senior leaders, academic staff and partners, acting as a trusted operational point of contact.\u00a0 Drive continuous improvement in service models, reporting, performance measures and ways of working across MNSD and partner teams.\u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a postgraduate qualification and extensive experience in large-scale project or operations management at corporate level.\u00a0 Operate effectively at senior management level, cultivating relationships and engaging diverse stakeholders in complex environments.\u00a0 Demonstrate strong expertise in governance, risk and compliance within large, complex or highly regulated organisations.\u00a0 Coordinate activity across multiple portfolios or functions, managing interdependencies to achieve whole-of-organisation outcomes.\u00a0 Produce clear, persuasive operational reports, briefings and advice for governance committees and executive audiences.\u00a0 Bring understanding of the defence and national security research environment, including associated risks, opportunities and compliance expectations.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u202f\u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u202f\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation\u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u202f\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning\u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u202fand well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave\u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u202f\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u202f\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u202f \u00a0 Your new team: You will join Melbourne Research and Enterprise within Chancellery, supporting the University\u2019s ambition to translate research excellence into societal benefit.\u00a0 Melbourne National Security and Defence brings together strategic governance, institutional coordination and partner engagement to strengthen the University\u2019s contribution to national security and defence. It connects expertise across the University, supports priority research capabilities and helps coordinate engagement with government, industry and higher education partners.\u00a0Working closely with the MNSD Executive Director and reporting to the Associate Director, Research Collaboration, you will help build the operational foundations of this initiative.\u00a0 \u00a0 Be Yourself \u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u202f\u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .\u202f\u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.\u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0\u00a0 You will need to be eligible to hold an appropriate Australian Government security clearance.\u00a0\u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.\u00a0\u00a0 You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this specific position, contact Michael Webb at m.webb@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at Alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0\u00a0 Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future.\u00a0 Applications close: \u200b Sunday 18 October 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.\u202f\u00a0 Position Description: \u202f\u00a0 PD_JR-019056 Operations Manager, Melbourne National Security and Defence.docx PD_JR-019056 Operations Manager, Melbourne National Security and Defence.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Senior-Advisor--Gender-Based-Violence_JR-017963-1": {"title": "Senior Advisor: Gender-Based Violence", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nPosition Number: JR-017963\u00a0\u00a0 Role type: Full-time; Continuing\u00a0 Department/School: Student and Scholarly Services\u00a0 Salary: UOM 8 - AUD $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Specialist trauma-informed role supporting victim survivors / shape University-wide gender-based violence responses / influence safer campus culture\u00a0 Apply to use your gender-based violence expertise to support our community and strengthen institutional responses.\u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will provide specialist, trauma-informed support to students and staff impacted by gender-based violence. You will guide colleagues and influence safer practices across the University\u2019s Safer Community Program. You will deepen your expertise applying national codes and risk frameworks in a complex, values-driven setting.\u00a0\u00a0 What you will deliver: \u00a0\u00a0 Provide trauma-informed responses to disclosures and reports of gender-based violence from students and staff.\u00a0 Connect impacted community members with specialist services and complaint processes within and external to the University.\u00a0 Assess risk, escalate urgent or complex matters, and contribute to coordinated safety planning across the University.\u00a0 Deliver capacity-building workshops and secondary consultations to strengthen colleagues\u2019 confidence responding to gender-based violence.\u00a0 Maintain high-quality, confidential case records and support continuous improvement of Safer Community Program practices.\u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0\u00a0 Hold a tertiary qualification in Social Work or an equivalent combination of education and experience.\u00a0 Demonstrate advanced, trauma-informed practice with victim survivors and understanding of support models for perpetrators.\u00a0 Bring strong knowledge of gender-based violence risk assessment, safety planning and relevant police and court processes.\u00a0 Understand the National Higher Education Code and university reporting and complaint processes, or quickly learn this context.\u00a0 Excel at building constructive relationships with diverse stakeholders in large, complex organisations.\u00a0 Produce clear, sensitive written documentation, including detailed case notes and accurate information management records.\u00a0 For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation\u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning\u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave\u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page! \u00a0 Your new team \u2013 Student and Scholarly Services \u00a0 You will join Student and Scholarly Services within the Chief Operating Officer Portfolio, a collaborative team that supports students from first enquiry to graduation. The Safer Community Program leads University-wide responses to concerning, threatening and harmful behaviours, partnering closely with faculties and central divisions. You will work with committed professionals who value evidence-informed practice, prevention and early intervention, and who are focused on building a safer, more respectful environment for every member of the University community.\u00a0\u00a0 Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .\u00a0\u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.\u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description below\u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email Tien Nguyen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0\u00a0 Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future.\u00a0 Applications close: Tuesday, 20th October 2026, at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0\u00a0 Position Description:\u00a0 \u00a0 PD_JR-017963 Senior Advisor Gender-Based Violence.docx \u00a0 PD_JR-017963 Senior Advisor Gender-Based Violence.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Fellow-in-Microbial-Fluid-Dynamics_JR-018985": {"title": "Research Fellow in Microbial Fluid Dynamics", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed term (2 years) Faculty: Science Department/School: School of Mathematics and Statistics Salary: Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super ARC-funded microbial fluid dynamics role / explore how flows shape microbial behaviour and signalling / join a top applied mathematics group. Apply now to drive pioneering microbial fluid dynamics research and contribute to world-class applied mathematics teaching. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will lead innovative research on microscale fluid dynamics that explains how flows shape microbial movement and communication. You will combine mathematical modelling, computation and collaboration to produce high-impact publications and support student learning. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Lead research on microbial fluid dynamics in unconfined and confined flows aligned with the ARC Future Fellowship. Develop and analyse mathematical and computational models of cell motility, navigation and micro-scale chemical interactions. Publish high-quality papers, present at conferences, and contribute to competitive research funding proposals. Teach one Applied Mathematics subject per year or equivalent, and support student supervision and mentoring. Engage with collaborators, participate in School activities, and promote the University\u2019s research profile and values. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a completed or near-completed PhD in Applied Mathematics or a closely related discipline. Demonstrate a strong research track record in areas aligned with fluid dynamics or cell motility. Bring experience in continuum or agent-based modelling and in developing computational simulations. Collaborate effectively within multidisciplinary teams and contribute positively to a collegial research culture. Communicate research clearly through publications, presentations, and engagement with diverse internal and external audiences. Offer desirable strengths such as high-performance computing, teaching experience or familiarity with experimental biophysics. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 School of Mathematics and Statistics The School of Mathematics and Statistics is one of Australia\u2019s premier mathematics communities, renowned for excellence in research, teaching and engagement. Home to a large, diverse cohort of academics, researchers and students, the School sustains a vibrant culture of seminars, collaborations and international partnerships. Within this, the Melbourne Applied Mathematics and Mathematical Biology Group brings together experts in fluid dynamics, mathematical biology and computation to tackle complex biological and physical systems. Supported by strong ARC funding and research infrastructure, the group offers an intellectually rich, collegial environment with deep connections across science and industry.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Douglas Brumley (details in PD) For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: 20 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 \u00a0 JR-018985 Research Fellow in Microbial Fluid Dynamics_PD.pdf JR-018985 Research Fellow in Microbial Fluid Dynamics_PD.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Fellow-in-Circular-Economy_JR-019034": {"title": "Research Fellow in Circular Economy", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time (1.0 FTE); Fixed term for up to 2 years Faculty: Engineering and Information Technology School: Chemical and Biomedical Engineering Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. (pro rata) plus 17% super\u00a0 Level B: AUD $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. (pro rata) plus 17% super\u00a0 Shape interdisciplinary circular economy research / collaborate with national and global partners / drive real-world sustainability impact Apply now to grow your research career while advancing circular economy practice and policy. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will work closely with the Chair of Circular Economy to design and deliver innovative research programmes. You will build partnerships across disciplines, industry and government to translate insights into measurable sustainability outcomes. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Design and deliver high-quality circular economy research projects in interdisciplinary teams with industry, government and campus partners. Identify and secure new collaborative funding opportunities, including proposals in plastics, metals, energy and related systems. Develop and manage project plans, budgets, timelines and milestones to ensure successful delivery of research programmes. Coordinate engagement activities including workshops, seminars and events that strengthen partnerships and visibility for circular economy. Contribute to advancing sustainability and circular economy at the University through publications, presentations and internal initiatives. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a PhD in a relevant field such as sustainability, circular economy or engineering. Demonstrate experience developing collaborative funding opportunities with partners in industry, government or research organisations. Work effectively within interdisciplinary research teams, building constructive relationships across diverse disciplines and stakeholder groups. Manage projects and budgets confidently, setting priorities, monitoring progress and delivering agreed outcomes on time. Communicate complex ideas clearly, with strong written, oral and workshop facilitation skills across in-person and online formats. Bring an outstanding track record in circular economy or related project areas, including independent, self-directed research (quantitative and/or qualitative). For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation. Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being.\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning. Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company.\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program.\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team Within the School of Chemical and Biomedical Engineering, you will join a vibrant, interdisciplinary research community. You will collaborate closely with the Department of Chemical Engineering, renowned for world-leading industry-engaged sustainability research. You will benefit from FEIT\u2019s strong partnerships, mission-led 2035 strategy and major circular economy research initiatives and centres. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Brett Verity at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Position Description: \u00a0 JR-019034 Research Fellow in Circular Economy PD.doc JR-019034 Research Fellow in Circular Economy PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Senior-Project-Officer_JR-018922": {"title": "Senior Project Officer", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u202fFull time; Fixed term for 12 months \u00a0 Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences\u00a0 Department/School: Department of Microbiology and Immunology, School of Biomedical Sciences\u00a0 Salary: $111,275 \u2013 $120,435 p.a. plus 17% super \u00a0 Impact global pathogen surveillance / coordinate platform deployments / collaborate with diverse public health partners \u00a0 Apply now to lead Trakka implementations and help transform pathogen genomics into actionable public health insight. \u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u202f \u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u202f\u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity \u202f\u00a0 You will coordinate platform deployments that enable real time pathogen genomics for critical public health decisions.\u00a0 You will partner with technical and public health teams, shaping how genomic data informs surveillance, research and biosecurity.\u00a0 You will gain experience working within a leading infection and immunity institute on nationally significant digital infrastructure. What you will deliver: \u202f \u00a0 Lead planning and coordination of platform deployments across various health settings. \u00a0 Develop and manage implementation plans, timelines, milestones and resources to support effective platform adoption. \u00a0 Coordinate stakeholder engagement, onboarding, training and user acceptance testing to ensure deployment readiness. \u00a0 Monitor project progress, manage risks and issues, and report outcomes, benefits and key performance indicators. \u00a0 Liaise between technical teams and end users, capturing feedback and supporting continuous platform improvement. \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u202f \u00a0 Hold a tertiary qualification in IT, data science, public health or a closely related discipline. \u00a0 Bring demonstrated experience managing complex projects, products or implementations involving IT systems or data platforms. \u00a0 Demonstrate strong stakeholder engagement skills and success building relationships across diverse partner organisations. \u00a0 Have experience working with information systems, digital platforms or data driven public health programmes. \u00a0 Apply excellent organisational, communication and problem solving capabilities within fast paced, multidisciplinary environments. \u00a0 Offer experience or strong interest in public health, genomics, bioinformatics or health data management contexts. \u00a0 For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u202f \u00a0 What we offer you! \u202f\u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u202f \u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation \u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u202f \u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning \u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u202fand well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. \u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u202f \u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u202f \u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u202f \u00a0 Your new team \u00a0 You will join the Department of Microbiology and Immunology within the School of Biomedical Sciences. Your role sits jointly in the Centre for Pathogen Genomics and the Microbiological Diagnostic Unit Public Health Laboratory. Together, these teams deliver integrated pathogen genomics, diagnostics and surveillance to support public health decision making. You will collaborate with leading experts across genomics, bioinformatics, epidemiology, laboratory science and capacity building. You will be part of an inclusive, values driven environment focused on innovation, impact and world health outcomes. \u00a0 \u00a0 Be Yourself \u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u202f\u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .\u00a0 \u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. \u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0 \u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0 \u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.\u00a0 \u00a0 You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application \u00a0 \u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this specific position, contact Tuyet Hoang at tuyet.hoang@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0 \u00a0 Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. \u00a0 Applications close: Sunday 8 November 1:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u202f \u00a0 Position Description: \u202f \u00a0 JR-018922 Senior Project Officer - PD.docx \u00a0 JR-018922 Senior Project Officer - PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Lecturer-in-Social-Work--Field-Education-_JR-018793-1": {"title": "Lecturer in Social Work (Field Education)", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u00a01 x Full Time, Continuing Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences School:\u00a0 School of Health Sciences Salary: Level B - $124,656 \u2013 $148,023 per annum p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Deliver outstanding social work field education / coordinate transformative MSW placements / create rich practice-based learning with diverse agencies Apply now to shape students\u2019 professional growth, building confident practitioners ready for evolving community needs. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will coordinate and enhance MSW field education, creating safe, high-quality and transformative practice learning experiences. You will work closely with students, educators and placement agencies to match learning opportunities to students\u2019 goals. You will contribute to teaching, innovation and scholarship that strengthen social work education and professional practice. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Develop and coordinate diverse social work placements that provide high-quality, safe and inclusive practice learning experiences. Facilitate student learning through supervision, liaison and tutorial visits, supporting students and placement educators in practice settings. Contribute to assessment and progression by undertaking placement visits, evaluating capabilities and addressing emerging learning needs. Support students experiencing placement challenges, negotiating solutions with agencies, supervisors and subject coordinators where required. Collaborate on field education subject delivery, integration seminars and innovations connecting academic learning with professional practice. \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a PhD or advanced social work practice profile and eligibility for Australian Association of Social Workers membership. Bring at least five years\u2019 post-qualification social work experience, ideally including supervisory, placement or educational responsibilities. Demonstrate excellence in teaching and student engagement, with commitment to innovative, continuously improving practice education approaches. Communicate clearly and collaboratively, building strong relationships with students, agencies, field educators and academic colleagues. Apply well-developed negotiation, conflict resolution and issue management skills in complex or sensitive placement situations. Contribute to research or scholarship in field education, professional learning or social work practice development. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team - You will join the Department of Social Work within the School of Health Sciences at the University of Melbourne. Our dynamic academic community is committed to social justice, evidence-informed practice and outstanding professional education. We partner with diverse health, community and human service organisations to deliver impactful placements and learning experiences. You will be supported by collegial peers and leaders who value collaboration, innovation and student-centred practice. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Milena Heinsch For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: 29 October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 JR-018793 Lecturer in Social Work (Field Education) PD.docx JR-018793 Lecturer in Social Work (Field Education) PD.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Aquarium-Technician_JR-019108-1": {"title": "Aquarium Technician", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing\u00a0 Department: ESG \u2013 Melbourne Bioresources - COO Portfolio\u00a0 Salary: UOM 3 - $69,554 - $76,795 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Work in a state-of-the-art zebrafish research facility / support world-class scientific discovery / develop specialist aquaculture and animal care skills\u00a0 Join a dedicated animal care team and apply your aquaculture skills to support impactful research.\u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u202f\u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity: \u00a0 In this Aquarium Technician role, you will ensure the health and welfare of zebrafish that underpin cutting-edge research. You will develop specialised aquaculture and laboratory animal skills while working in a highly collaborative, research-focused environment.\u00a0 What you will deliver: \u00a0 Provide daily husbandry, feeding, cleaning and health monitoring of zebrafish to the highest animal welfare standards.\u00a0 Perform routine tank cleaning, facility hygiene tasks and basic equipment care to ensure reliable aquarium operations.\u00a0 Conduct regular water quality testing, record results accurately and report issues promptly to support stable system performance.\u00a0 Maintain breeding colonies and live food cultures to support research requirements and colony health.\u00a0 Adhere to regulatory and ethical requirements, including AWV, POCTA, OGTR and DAFF, in all facility activities.\u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold completed Year 12 and a Certificate II in aquaculture, or equivalent relevant experience and training.\u00a0 Demonstrate strong attention to detail, clear communication skills and reliable record-keeping in technical or animal care settings.\u00a0 Apply sound organisational skills to manage routine tasks efficiently and follow established standard operating procedures independently.\u00a0 Collaborate effectively within a small technical team to support consistent facility operations and animal welfare outcomes.\u00a0 Bring experience with recirculating aquaculture systems, equipment maintenance or aquatic animal health monitoring, or similar environments.\u00a0 Offer prior experience working in a laboratory animal facility or regulated animal research environment.\u00a0 Are available to undertake occasional weekend work as required to support facility operations.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u202f\u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u202f\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation\u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u202f\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning\u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u202f\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u202f\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u202f \u00a0 Your new team: \u00a0 Within the Chief Operating Officer Portfolio, you will join the Melbourne Bioresources platform in the state-of-the-art Zebrafish Aquarium. The team enables high-impact research by providing exceptional animal care, technical support and reliable aquatic systems for University of Melbourne researchers. You will be part of a collaborative, detail-focused group that values animal welfare, continuous improvement and developing specialist skills in aquaculture and laboratory animal science.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u202f\u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .\u202f\u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.\u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0\u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.\u00a0\u00a0 You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application.\u00a0\u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at Alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0\u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0\u00a0 Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future.\u00a0 Applications close: \u200b Sunday 25 October 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.\u202f\u00a0 Position Description: \u202f PD_JR-019108 Aquarium Technician .docx\u00a0 PD_JR-019108 Aquarium Technician .pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Assistant-to-Head-of-Department_JR-019137": {"title": "Research Assistant to Head of Department", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Part-time/ Full Time; 0.5 \u2013 1 FTE; Fixed-term for 5 months Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/ School: Department of Medical Education, Melbourne Medical School Salary: Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. pro rata for part time plus 17% super Work alongside a senior Head of Department / deepen qualitative and quantitative research expertise / contribute to medical education impact Apply your research skills in medical education and grow your academic profile in a supportive team Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will work closely with the Head of Department on high-quality medical education research projects. You will strengthen your qualitative and quantitative analysis skills while contributing to publications, presentations and curriculum innovation. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Provide research and administrative support to the Head of Department across multiple medical education projects. Conduct qualitative analyses and support quantitative analyses of administrative, longitudinal and linked assessment datasets. Manage, assemble and clean datasets, ensuring data quality for reporting, manuscripts and conference presentations. Coordinate ethics applications and processes, and maintain productive relationships with project partners and stakeholders. Conduct literature searches and systematic reviews, and contribute to written reports, papers and presentations. \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold an undergraduate degree in a relevant discipline and an interest in medical education research. Use qualitative analysis software such as NVivo and manage survey data using platforms like Qualtrics. Demonstrate strong skills in literature review, synthesis of peer-reviewed and grey literature, and academic writing. Understand human research ethics principles and have experience preparing and managing ethics submissions. Bring excellent attention to detail, diligence and organisational skills to manage concurrent research tasks and deadlines. Value collaborative work in multidisciplinary teams and communicate effectively with diverse colleagues and stakeholders. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 You will join the Department of Medical Education within the Melbourne Medical School at Parkville. The Department leads the Doctor of Medicine curriculum, postgraduate programmes and research that improves health professional education. Research spans work integrated learning, curriculum and assessment, healthcare communication and student engagement. You will collaborate with academics, clinicians and professional staff in a supportive, values-driven environment encouraging innovation. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Anna Ryan by emailing annatr@unimelb.edu.au. For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: 29 October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 JR-019137 Research Assistant to Head of Department PD.docx JR-019137 Research Assistant to Head of Department PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Assistant_JR-018720": {"title": "Research Assistant in Sleep and Daily Rhythms", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term (1 year) Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department: Centre for Youth Mental Health Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super Work in a world-leading youth mental health centre / drive NHMRC-funded LUMOS sleep\u2013circadian research / shape early psychosis detection and care Apply now to support young people at risk of psychosis through rigorous sleep and circadian research Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. \u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will support the LUMOS study, tracking sleep and circadian rhythms in youth psychosis. You will gain hands-on experience in clinical interviewing, neurocognitive assessment and high-quality longitudinal research. You will work within a multidisciplinary team at Orygen, translating discoveries into better early intervention decisions. Occasional off-site (e.g. at Orygen headspace centres) visits to participants, regular attendance at the study sites, and some out of hours work will be required. What you will deliver: Recruit and enrol young participants into the LUMOS sleep and circadian study across clinical services. Conduct structured clinical interviews and neurocognitive assessments, including pupil measures and computerised cognitive tasks. Maintain accurate, confidential, audit-ready participant records and study databases in line with Good Clinical Practice guidelines. Liaise with clinical teams, young people and families to support recruitment, follow-up and engagement over 12 months. Assist with ethics and governance submissions, standard operating procedures, manuscripts and presentations for multidisciplinary project meetings. You may be a great fit if you: Hold an honours-level degree in behavioural or social sciences, or equivalent education and relevant experience. Demonstrate strong interest in youth mental health and sound understanding of psychiatric diagnoses and disorders. Conduct structured interviews and engage sensitively with young people experiencing mental health difficulties. Follow complex research protocols, maintain precise records and troubleshoot issues in fast-paced clinical research settings. Use electronic data capture platforms, preferably REDCap, for secure entry, verification and basic data analysis. Manage workloads independently across multiple sites, collaborating with multidisciplinary stakeholders and meeting competing deadlines. For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team \u2013 Centre for Youth Mental Health At the Centre for Youth Mental Health, you will join a pioneering leader in youth mental health research. Orygen\u2019s multidisciplinary teams integrate clinical services, research and knowledge translation to improve outcomes for young people. You will work alongside clinicians, scientists and allied professionals within Australia\u2019s largest health sciences faculty. The team values curiosity, rigour, collaboration and genuine impact on early intervention and prevention in psychosis. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process . Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Dr Mag de Rozario at mag.derozario@unimelb.edu.au . For questions related to our recruitment process email Vanessa Phan at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: 29 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: PD_JR-018720 Research Assistant in Sleep and Daily Rhythms.docx PD_JR-018720 Research Assistant in Sleep and Daily Rhythms.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/The-Director--Enterprise-Partnerships--Health-and-Life-Sciences-_JR-019078": {"title": "Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Health and Life Sciences)", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nDirector, Enterprise Partnerships (Health and Life Sciences) Melbourne Enterprise | Melbourne Research and Enterprise Portfolio Full-time | Fixed term for 5 years The University of Melbourne is home to some of Australia's most significant research, innovation and entrepreneurial capabilities, with a track record that includes strategic agreements with leading global organisations. This is a significant opportunity to build on that foundation, grow the University's external partnerships and unlock new opportunities across the health and life sciences sector. We are seeking an exceptional senior leader to lead Enterprise Partnerships across health and life sciences. Reporting to the Executive Director, Partnerships and Precincts, you will identify, shape and convert strategic opportunities that connect the University's world-class capabilities with industry, government, health services, medical research institutes and other key partners. As a senior institutional interface, you will bring together expertise from across the University to develop high-impact partnerships that accelerate research translation, support improved health outcomes, build workforce capability, influence policy and generate lasting economic and societal impact. The opportunity This is a career-defining opportunity to build on a strong foundation of global partnerships and shape the next generation of health and life sciences engagement at one of the world's leading universities. Building on a strong track record of strategic agreements that are among the first of their kind in Australia, you will have the opportunity to develop new partnerships and extend the University's impact across Australia and internationally. You will lead strategic partnership development, business development and key account management across the sector, building trusted relationships with senior external stakeholders and translating the University's research, education, talent and innovation capabilities into compelling partner propositions. You will also strengthen a more market-facing, sector-led and partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, while leading and developing a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. Key responsibilities include: Originate, shape and convert high-value strategic partnerships, collaborations and commercial opportunities across the health and life sciences sector. Build influential relationships with senior leaders across industry, government, healthcare, medical research and innovation, both nationally and internationally. Translate the University's research, education, talent, commercialisation and enterprise capabilities into compelling value propositions for partners. Identify and prioritise a strong pipeline of opportunities, applying commercial judgement, governance and disciplined account and performance management. Mobilise multidisciplinary expertise across faculties, research institutes and professional services to respond to complex opportunities and maximise impact. Strengthen a sector-led, partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, including data-informed decision-making and effective use of CRM and emerging technologies. Lead and develop a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. About you You are an accomplished partnerships, commercial, business development or enterprise leader with deep knowledge of the health and life sciences sector and a strong track record of creating and growing complex, high-value partnerships. You bring strategic insight, commercial acumen and exceptional relationship-building capability, with the credibility to engage senior stakeholders, navigate complex environments and turn opportunities into meaningful outcomes. You will bring: Significant experience leading strategic partnerships, business development or commercial activity within industry, government, healthcare, research or innovation environments. Deep knowledge of the health and life sciences sector, with the ability to identify emerging trends across biotechnology, medical technologies, pharmaceuticals, precision medicine, research translation and related fields and translate these into strategic partnership opportunities. Experience working within, or closely alongside, research-intensive, innovation-led or knowledge-based organisations, with the ability to connect complex research capability with external partner needs. A strong track record of establishing and growing influential relationships across industry, government, healthcare, research and innovation ecosystems and converting these into strategic opportunities. Demonstrated commercial judgement in structuring complex partnerships, evaluating opportunities, managing risk and navigating governance to achieve sustainable outcomes. Experience shaping new approaches, capabilities or operating models in complex environments and bringing diverse stakeholders with you. Outstanding communication, influencing and stakeholder engagement skills, with the judgement and credibility to build alignment and influence senior decision-makers. A collaborative and inclusive leadership style, with experience setting direction, building capability and developing high-performing teams. How to apply To apply, please submit: A current CV. A cover letter (maximum two pages) outlining your suitability for the role. For role or application process enquiries, please contact Lucy Porter , Executive Recruitment Partner, at exec-recruitment@unimelb.edu.au . If you are ready to build on world-class research and innovation capabilities, grow high-impact partnerships and shape lasting health, economic and societal impact, we invite you to apply. Applications must be submitted through the online application portal and will not be accepted by email. Please note: The University is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Applications close: Monday 9th November 2026 at 11:55pm Melbourne time. \u00a0 Position Description: PD - Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Health, LS).pdf PD - Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Health, LS).docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Director--Partnership-Success-Group_JR-019079": {"title": "Director, Partnership Success Group", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nDirector, Partnership Success Group Melbourne Enterprise | Melbourne Research and Enterprise Portfolio Full-time | Fixed term for 5 years The University of Melbourne is one of the world's leading research-intensive universities, with the research capability, talent and enterprise ambition to solve society's most significant challenges. Melbourne Enterprise is building a more coordinated, market-facing and partner-centric institution, connecting world-class research and expertise with industry, government and other external partners. We are seeking an exceptional senior leader to lead the Partnership Success Group, a pivotal enterprise capability that will shape how the University mobilises, delivers and deepens partnerships across research, innovation and workforce development. Reporting to the Executive Director, Partnerships and Precincts, you will bring together commercial development, market intelligence and systems, partnership mobilisation and delivery, workforce capability and organisational capability development to enable partnership growth at scale. As a key institutional leader, you will work across faculties, Melbourne Enterprise and professional services to mobilise expertise and resources around complex opportunities, strengthen commercial and delivery capability, and create a consistently exceptional partner experience. You will play a central role in establishing the systems, frameworks, governance and ways of working that enable a consistent, market-facing and partner-centric approach to partnering across the University. The opportunity This is a career-defining opportunity to build enterprise capability that will shape how one of the world's leading universities develops, mobilises and delivers partnerships at scale. You will lead the Partnership Success Group, strengthening the University's ability to shape opportunities, structure partnerships and deliver them effectively. Working closely with sector-aligned Enterprise Partnerships leaders and colleagues across the University, you will bring together commercial, delivery and institutional capability to turn major opportunities into enduring partnerships and measurable impact. You will also lead the University's workforce capability partnering function, bringing together professional education, custom education and industry talent solutions into a coordinated enterprise offering that connects partners with the University's education, workforce and talent capabilities, including students, PhDs and graduates. A key focus will be building the systems, intelligence and organisational capability that enable better decision-making, effective prioritisation, strong partnership performance and an exceptional experience for partners. Key responsibilities include: Establish and lead the Partnership Success Group, building the commercial, delivery and organisational capability required to mobilise and deliver partnerships at scale. Lead the University's workforce capability partnering function, aligning professional education, custom education and industry talent solutions with partner needs and institutional priorities. Provide strategic leadership for partnership mobilisation and delivery, ensuring major opportunities and partnerships are governed effectively, executed consistently and deliver value for partners and the University. Lead the uplift of partnership development capability across research, innovation and workforce development, including opportunity shaping, business case development, partnership structuring and commercial frameworks. Establish and enhance partner intelligence, CRM, pipeline management and reporting capabilities to support effective prioritisation, resource allocation and decision-making. Mobilise cross-functional teams and institutional resources around complex opportunities, working across faculties and professional services to maximise partnership outcomes. Establish best-practice frameworks, standards and tools that embed consistent ways of working and build enterprise-wide partnership capability. About you You are an accomplished partnerships, commercial, business development or enterprise transformation leader with a strong track record of building capability, leading complex functions and converting opportunities into sustainable organisational, commercial or societal outcomes. You bring strategic insight, commercial acumen and exceptional stakeholder engagement capability, with the credibility to work across a complex, matrixed organisation and influence senior executives, academic leaders, professional teams and external partners. You will bring: Significant experience leading strategic growth, business development, partnership delivery or enterprise transformation functions, with a track record of converting opportunities into sustainable outcomes. Demonstrated ability to mobilise diverse functions around large-scale, complex and high-value opportunities, bringing together commercial, delivery, technical and stakeholder capability to achieve institutional outcomes. Strong technology-enabled leadership, championing digital innovation, human-centred design and data-driven approaches to partner engagement, pipeline management and growth. An ability to create the conditions and environment for innovation. Strong commercial acumen, including experience developing business cases, shaping partnership models, structuring opportunities and navigating financial, governance, risk and delivery considerations. Proven experience establishing, leading or transforming complex functions, operating models or organisational capabilities, including embedding new ways of working and leading organisational change. Exceptional stakeholder engagement, influencing and negotiation skills, with the ability to build credibility and trust with senior executives, academic leaders, professional teams and external partners. Highly developed strategic judgement and problem-solving skills, with the agility to operate in ambiguity, balance competing priorities and make sound decisions at pace. A collaborative and influential leadership style, with the ability to break down organisational silos, align diverse stakeholders around shared outcomes and build trusted relationships across the University. How to apply To apply, please submit: A current CV. A cover letter (maximum two pages) outlining your suitability for the role. For role or application process enquiries, please contact Lucy Porter , Executive Recruitment Partner, at exec-recruitment@unimelb.edu.au . If you are ready to build enterprise capability that will shape how the University develops, delivers and deepens partnerships at scale, we invite you to apply. Applications must be submitted through the online application portal and will not be accepted by email. Please note: The University is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Applications close: Monday 2nd November 2026 at 11:55pm Melbourne time. Position Description: PD - JR-019079 Director, Partnership Success Group.pdf PD - JR-019079 Director, Partnership Success Group.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Deputy-Director-of-Indigenous-Medical-Education_JR-018456-1": {"title": "Deputy Director of Indigenous Medical Education", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nRole type: \u00a0Full Time; Continuing Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department: Department of Medical Education Salary: Level B $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super\u00a0 Shape Indigenous medical education across Melbourne\u2019s Doctor of Medicine program through curriculum innovation, meaningful partnerships and culturally safe practice to improve First Nations health outcomes. Apply now to drive strategic curriculum leadership, evidence-informed teaching and meaningful community engagement in one of Australia\u2019s leading medical schools. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as these positions are exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic).\u202f The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 Help shape the next generation of doctors through leadership in Indigenous medical education. You will guide curriculum innovation, assessment and community engagement initiatives that advance culturally safe practice and contribute to improved First Nations health outcomes. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Provide leadership for the development and delivery of First Nations health education across the Doctor of Medicine program, ensuring alignment with learning outcomes, curriculum priorities and accreditation requirements. Drive the design and continuous improvement of culturally safe, evidence-informed learning and assessment practices, including innovative teaching resources, placements and digital learning experiences. Support and enable high-performing teams and partnerships, fostering collaboration across academic, clinical and community settings to achieve impactful educational outcomes. Strengthen engagement with First Nations communities and health organisations, ensuring Indigenous knowledges, perspectives and priorities inform curriculum, educational practice and quality improvement activities. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a tertiary qualification in health, education, public health or an equivalent combination of training. Bring experience in health professional education, with expertise in curriculum design, assessment and educational quality improvement in a tertiary or healthcare setting. Demonstrate leadership and collaboration skills, with the ability to influence practice, support teams and work effectively across complex organisational environments. Build strong and respectful relationships with diverse stakeholders, including First Nations communities, health services, academic colleagues and partner organisations. Have a strong understanding of First Nations health, cultural safety and health equity, together with a commitment to improving outcomes for First Nations peoples through education. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 \u201cThe Wurru Wurru Health Unit\u201d \u00a0 At the Wurru Wurru Health Unit, we are shaping the future of medical education. Through partnerships with First Nations communities, clinicians and educators we lead the design and delivery of First Nations health education that prepares future doctors to provide culturally safe, responsive and equitable care. As part of our team, you will have the opportunity to influence one of Australia's leading medical programs, contribute to sector-leading curriculum innovation, and work alongside passionate colleagues dedicated to advancing First Nations health and educational excellence. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the our recruitment process, including application tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A valid Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please submit: A current resume (CV) A cover letter outlining your interest in the role and relevant experience Your responses to the selection criteria, found in the position description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Dr Bridie Hofstee at b.hofstee@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process, contact Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments, contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: 8 November 2026 11:55 PM; AEDT.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 JR-018456 Deputy Director of Indigenous Medical Education PD.docx JR-018456 Deputy Director of Indigenous Medical Education PD.pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Director--Enterprise-Partnerships--Energy--Primary-Industries---Education-_JR-019080": {"title": "Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Energy, Primary Industries & Education)", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nDirector, Enterprise Partnerships (Energy, Primary Industries & Education) Melbourne Enterprise | Melbourne Research and Enterprise Portfolio Continuing | Fixed-term for 5 years The University of Melbourne is home to some of Australia's most significant research, innovation and entrepreneurial capabilities, with a track record that includes strategic agreements with leading global organisations. This is a significant opportunity to build on that foundation, grow the University's external partnerships and unlock new opportunities across energy, primary industries and education. We are seeking an exceptional senior leader to lead Enterprise Partnerships across energy, primary industries and education. Reporting to the Executive Director, Partnerships and Precincts, you will identify, shape and convert strategic opportunities that connect the University's world-class capabilities with industry, government, primary industries, education providers and other key partners. As a senior institutional interface, you will bring together expertise from across the University to develop high-impact partnerships that accelerate the energy transition, strengthen sustainable and productive primary industries, build workforce capability, influence policy and generate lasting economic and societal impact. The opportunity This is a career-defining opportunity to build on a strong foundation of global partnerships and shape the next generation of engagement across energy, primary industries and education at one of the world's leading universities. Building on a strong track record of strategic agreements that are among the first of their kind in Australia, you will have the opportunity to develop new partnerships and extend the University's impact across Australia and internationally. You will lead strategic partnership development, business development and key account management across the sectors, building trusted relationships with senior external stakeholders and translating the University's research, education, talent and innovation capabilities into compelling partner propositions. You will also strengthen a more market-facing, sector-led and partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, while leading and developing a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. Key responsibilities include: Originate, shape and convert high-value strategic partnerships, collaborations and commercial opportunities across energy, primary industries and education. Build influential relationships with senior leaders across industry, government, research, education, primary industries and innovation, both nationally and internationally. Translate the University's research, education, talent, commercialisation and enterprise capabilities into compelling value propositions for partners. Identify and prioritise a strong pipeline of opportunities, applying commercial judgement, governance and disciplined account and performance management. Mobilise multidisciplinary expertise across faculties, research institutes and professional services to respond to complex opportunities and maximise impact. Strengthen a sector-led, partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, including data-informed decision-making and effective use of CRM and emerging technologies. Lead and develop a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. About you You are an accomplished partnerships, commercial, business development or enterprise leader with deep knowledge of the energy, primary industries and/or education sectors and a strong track record of creating and growing complex, high-value partnerships. You bring strategic insight, commercial acumen and exceptional relationship-building capability, with the credibility to engage senior stakeholders, navigate complex environments and turn opportunities into meaningful outcomes. You will bring: Significant experience leading strategic partnerships, business development or commercial activity within industry, government, research, education, primary industries or innovation environments. Deep knowledge of the energy, primary industries and/or education landscape, with the ability to identify emerging trends across energy transition, climate resilience, biodiversity and environmental sustainability, sustainable agriculture and food systems, workforce development, educational innovation and lifelong learning, and translate these into strategic partnership opportunities. Experience working within, or closely alongside, research-intensive, innovation-led or knowledge-based organisations, with the ability to connect complex research capability with external partner needs. A strong track record of establishing and growing influential relationships across industry, government, research, education, sustainability and innovation ecosystems and converting these into strategic opportunities. Demonstrated commercial judgement in structuring complex partnerships, evaluating opportunities, managing risk and navigating governance to achieve sustainable outcomes. Experience shaping new approaches, capabilities or operating models in complex environments and bringing diverse stakeholders with you. Outstanding communication, influencing and stakeholder engagement skills, with the judgement and credibility to build alignment and influence senior decision-makers. A collaborative and inclusive leadership style, with experience setting direction, building capability and developing high-performing teams. How to apply To apply, please submit: A current CV. A cover letter (maximum two pages) outlining your suitability for the role. For role or application process enquiries, please contact Lucy Porter, Executive Recruitment Partner, at exec-recruitment@unimelb.edu.au . If you are ready to build on world-class research and innovation capabilities, grow high-impact partnerships and shape lasting environmental, economic, workforce and societal impact, we invite you to apply. Applications must be submitted through the online application portal and will not be accepted by email. Please note: The University is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Applications close: Monday 2nd November at 11:55pm Melbourne time. Position Description: PD - JR-019080 Director Enterprise Partnerships (Energy, Primary Industries, Education).pdf PD - JR-019080 Director Enterprise Partnerships (Energy, Primary Industries, Education).docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Brisbane-Queensland/Manager---Ecosystem-Partnerships---Engagement--QLD--NSW---ACT-_JR-018830": {"title": "Manager \u2013 Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (QLD, NSW & ACT)", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part Time (0.6FTE); Fixed Term for 12 months Faculty: Faculty of Business & Economics Location : Brisbane, Queensland Department:\u00a0 Dilin Duwa Centre for Indigenous Business Leadership Salary: $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. pro-rata for part time plus 17% super\u00a0 Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic). Strategic Indigenous ecosystem leadership across QLD, NSW and ACT / community and partner engagement / grow impact through funded initiative Join Dilin Duwa to strengthen Indigenous business networks and lead high impact partnerships across QLD, NSW and ACT. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will lead ecosystem partnerships and engagement across eastern Australia, connecting Indigenous businesses, communities and partners. You will translate relationships into new programmes, initiatives and revenue that grow Dilin Duwa\u2019s reach and impact. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Lead regional engagement initiatives, workshops and dialogues that are culturally grounded and aligned with Dilin Duwa. Build and manage strategic partnerships across the Indigenous business ecosystem, including corporations, government, universities and philanthropy. Drive business development opportunities, developing proposals, funding submissions and enterprise services that expand contracted revenue. Strengthen alumni, leadership and regional networks of Indigenous entrepreneurs and organisations connected to Dilin Duwa. Oversee programme delivery and partnerships from planning through evaluation, monitoring outcomes and reporting insights for improvement. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a relevant postgraduate qualification or equivalent senior experience in a related field. Be of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent and bring lived experience to this work. Demonstrate strong experience working with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities or organisations. Build and sustain complex relationships and partnerships across community, corporate, government and ecosystem stakeholders. Manage programs and projects end-to-end, with strong organisational skills and high-level project management capability. Use Microsoft Office confidently and adapt quickly to new software, systems and digital engagement tools. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team Dilin Duwa is a pioneering Centre for Indigenous Business Leadership within the Faculty of Business and Economics. The Centre partners with Melbourne Business School to deliver research, education and ecosystem services nationally. You will join a collaborative team committed to Indigenous economic independence and thriving Indigenous enterprises. The team values culturally grounded practice, deep community engagement and evidence based impact. Based in Brisbane, you will connect regional ecosystems with Dilin Duwa\u2019s national networks and capabilities.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Paul Paton by emailing p.paton@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Peterson at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Tuesday 27 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 Word\u00a0: Manager - Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (QLD, NSW & ACT)_PD (1).docx PDF\u00a0: Manager - Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (QLD, NSW & ACT)_PD (1).pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Perth-Western-Australia/Manager---Ecosystem-Partnerships---Engagement--WA--SA---NT-_JR-018829-1": {"title": "Manager \u2013 Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (WA, SA & NT)", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part-time (0.6 FTE); Fixed-term, 12 months \u00a0 Faculty: Business and Economics Location: Perth, Western Australia Department/School: Dilin Duwa Centre for Indigenous Business Leadership Salary: AUD $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. (pro-rata) plus 17% super\u00a0 Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic). Strategic Indigenous ecosystem leadership across WA, SA and NT / community and partner engagement / grow impact through funded initiative Join Dilin Duwa to strengthen Indigenous business networks and lead high impact partnerships across WA, SA and NT. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will lead ecosystem partnerships and engagement for Dilin Duwa across WA, SA and NT. You will build culturally grounded relationships with Indigenous communities and partners, shaping programs that grow economic independence. You will translate networks into new initiatives, collaborations and revenue streams that expand the Centre\u2019s national impact. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Lead culturally grounded engagement initiatives, regional workshops and leadership programs with Indigenous communities and partners across the region. Develop and manage strategic partnerships with Indigenous business ecosystem organisations, government, corporates, universities and philanthropic stakeholders. Drive business development by identifying enterprise service opportunities, preparing proposals and funding submissions, and growing contracted revenue. Expand Dilin Duwa\u2019s alumni and leadership networks, strengthening national connections among Indigenous entrepreneurs and organisations. Oversee project delivery, contractors and facilitators, monitoring outcomes and reporting on program impact, risks and improvements. \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a relevant postgraduate qualification or bring equivalent senior experience in a related field. Be of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent and bring lived experience to this work. Demonstrate strong experience working with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities or organisations on programs or initiatives. Bring proven capability in relationship building, partnership development, stakeholder engagement and business development across diverse sectors. Manage programs or projects with high level organisational skills, reporting on outcomes, risks and impact. Use Microsoft Office confidently and quickly learn new software to support program, partnership and reporting activities. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team Dilin Duwa is a pioneering Centre for Indigenous Business Leadership within the Faculty of Business and Economics. The Centre partners with Melbourne Business School to deliver research, education and ecosystem services nationally. You will join a collaborative team committed to Indigenous economic independence and thriving Indigenous enterprises. The team values culturally grounded practice, deep community engagement and evidence based impact. Based in Perth, you will connect regional ecosystems with Dilin Duwa\u2019s national networks and capabilities.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Paul Paton at p.paton@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: Tuesday 27 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: Word\u00a0: Manager - Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (WA, SA & NT)_PD (1).docx PDF\u00a0: Manager - Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (WA, SA & NT)_PD (1).pdf"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Graduate-Researcher-Academic-Associate-in-History-and-Theory_JR-019174": {"title": "Graduate Researcher Academic Associate in History and Theory", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nJob no: several new positions Location:\u00a0 Parkville Role type:\u00a0 0.2 FTE Part-time Fixed-term for 24-months, tied to candidature and valid work rights Faculty:\u00a0 Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning Salary:\u00a0 Level A.2\u00a0\u2013 $92,243 per annum, pro rata for part-time, plus 17% super \u00a0 New opportunity to gain meaningful academic experience in education in the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning Undertake career advancing professional development, including 30 hours paid professional development Salary packaging, subsidised health and wellbeing services, fitness and cultural clubs, Myki discounts, and a 25% discount on graduate courses to our staff and their immediate families \u00a0 About the Role The Faculty invites applications for several new Graduate Researcher Academic Associate (GRAA) positions from graduate researchers currently enrolled in a PhD in the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning with discipline and domain knowledge in the subjects comprising the Undergraduate Bachelor of Design with a focus in built environment history and theory taught in the Architecture Major.\u00a0 \u00a0 As a GRAA in History and Theory you will contribute positively, both individually and as part of a team, to educating, inspiring and supporting students. The GRAAs will be responsible for carrying out education related activity for one or more subjects. Key education responsibilities will include: \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Teaching, including through tutorials, seminars, workshops \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Undertaking preparation and associated work, including teaching and student consultation \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Regular consultation with students, monitor discussion boards/forums, respond to queries, conduct exam revision sessions \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Marking a share of project assessment submissions \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Managing student submissions (e.g. chasing up missing submissions, handling late submissions, handling remark requests, assessment feedback)] \u00a0 In addition, you will be supported to participate as an academic member of the Faculty in contributing to the Faculty\u2019s academic mission, life and community and supported to engage in at least 30 hours of paid professional development. \u00a0 Who We Are Looking For We are seeking applicants enrolled as a University of Melbourne student undertaking a PhD in the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning with discipline knowledge in built environment history and theory You will be a passionate educator and have the ability to contribute positively, both individually and as part of a team, to educating, inspiring and supporting students. You will also have excellent written and verbal communication and interpersonal skills and excellent organisational skills with the ability to manage one\u2019s own time effectively, administer multiple tasks simultaneously and meet deadlines. For further information regarding responsibilities and requirements, please refer to the attached PD. Position Description JR-019174 Graduate Research Academic Associate in History and Theory PD.docx JR-019174 Graduate Research Academic Associate in History and Theory PD.pdf This role requires that the successful candidate must undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check. This is essential to guarantee a secure environment for all individuals at the University. In the case the selected candidate doesn't have a valid Working with Children Check, they will be able to apply for the WWCC as part of the initial onboarding procedures."}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Academic-Tutor---Murrup-Barak_JR-018018": {"title": "Academic Tutor - Murrup Barak", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nRole type: \u202f Part-time (minimum 0.2 FTE); Fixed term\u00a0 Department/ School:\u202f \u202fIndigenous Portfolio \u2013 Murrup Barak\u00a0 Salary: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. (pro rata for part-time) plus 17% super\u202f\u00a0 Make a direct impact on Indigenous student success / share real-world discipline expertise / join a supportive Indigenous-led team\u00a0 Apply now to use your discipline expertise to empower Indigenous students through tailored, high-impact tutoring.\u00a0 Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u202f\u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. \u00a0 Your next career opportunity \u202f\u00a0 You will provide focused academic tutoring that strengthens success and confidence for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students.\u00a0 You will join a collaborative team of tutors, shaping culturally safe, empowering learning experiences across diverse disciplines.\u00a0 What you will deliver: \u202f\u00a0 Design and deliver weekly discipline-specific tutoring sessions, individually and in groups, to support student learning goals.\u00a0 Guide students to develop self-directed learning strategies while maintaining strong academic standards and integrity.\u00a0 Collaborate with fellow tutors to create, refine and share discipline resources and participate in professional development.\u00a0 Build culturally safe, supportive learning environments and make referrals to the Student Success Team when needed.\u00a0 Maintain accurate tutoring records, schedules and progress notes, ensuring program systems are used effectively.\u00a0 \u00a0 You may be a great fit if you: \u202f\u00a0 Hold an undergraduate degree and strong knowledge of your discipline at postgraduate level.\u00a0 Bring experience supporting or understanding Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students in tertiary learning environments.\u00a0 Demonstrate awareness of systemic barriers affecting Indigenous students and commitment to culturally safe practice.\u00a0 Coach learners in complex concepts, adapting explanations for different levels and assessment expectations.\u00a0 Manage tutoring schedules reliably, using program systems and processes to track attendance and progress.\u00a0 Collaborate effectively within a tutor team and with Student Success colleagues to support student wellbeing.\u00a0 For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u202f\u00a0 What we offer you! \u202f\u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u202f\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation\u00a0 Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u202f\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning\u00a0 Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u202fand well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.\u00a0 Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u202f\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u202f\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u202f \u00a0 Your new team \u00a0 You will join Murrup Barak, the Indigenous student services hub within the University\u2019s Indigenous Portfolio.\u00a0 The team collaborates across the University to provide safe, empowering support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students.\u00a0 You will be part of a growing tutor cohort, valued for discipline expertise, cultural respect and student-centred practice.\u00a0 Be Yourself \u202f\u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. \u00a0 We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. \u00a0 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u202f\u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .\u00a0\u00a0 Application essentials: \u00a0 Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.\u00a0 You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.\u00a0\u00a0 A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.\u00a0\u00a0 Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.\u00a0\u00a0 You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application.\u00a0\u00a0 Want to know more? \u00a0 For queries related to this specific position, contact Josh Cubillo at josh.cubillo@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au \u00a0 For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').\u00a0\u00a0 Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Applications close: Tuesday 27th October11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u202f\u00a0 Position Description: \u202f JR-018018 Academic Tutor - Murrup Barak - PD.pdf \u00a0 JR-018018 Academic Tutor - Murrup Barak - PD.docx"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Fellow-in-Mathematics_JR-018338": {"title": "Research Fellow in Mathematics", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed term (2 years) Faculty: Science School : School of Mathematics and Statistics Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super ARC-funded Langlands research role / strong emphasis on high-quality mathematical publications / collaborative, internationally connected research environment Apply now to drive cutting-edge research in representation theory and build a strong independent research profile. Investing in you \u2013 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.\u00a0 The University of Melbourne \u00a0 We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity \u00a0 You will contribute to an ARC-funded project at the forefront of the Langlands program. You will develop your own research agenda in algebraic geometry and representation theory within a supportive team. You will gain mentoring, collaboration opportunities and a strong platform for your future academic career. What you will deliver: \u00a0 Conduct internationally competitive research on representation theory and Langlands-related topics, leading to high quality journal publications. Collaborate closely with Chief Investigators to achieve ARC Discovery Project milestones and contribute to team research outputs. Present research at seminars and conferences, and engage with broader mathematical communities locally and internationally. Contribute to supervision and mentoring of postgraduate and research students in your area of expertise. Assist with preparing funding proposals, managing project administration and meeting reporting requirements for the research program. You may be a great fit if you: \u00a0 Hold a completed or submitted PhD in mathematics. Demonstrate strong expertise in algebraic geometry and representation theory. Bring experience assisting with supervision of undergraduate or higher degree research projects. Show capacity to design, manage and complete research projects with limited supervision. Communicate research clearly through high-quality written work and effective presentations in English. Value collaborative, inclusive teamwork and thrive in diverse, international mathematical environments. For further information please refer to the attached PD.\u00a0 What we offer you! \u00a0 We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:\u00a0 Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being\u00a0 Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance\u00a0and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company\u00a0 Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program\u00a0 For more information check out our benefits page!\u00a0 Your new team \u2013 School of Mathematics and Statistics \u00a0 The School of Mathematics and Statistics is one of Australia\u2019s leading centres for mathematical research and teaching. It hosts major ARC centres and fellowships, creating a vibrant hub for advanced work in algebraic geometry and representation theory. Within the Faculty of Science, the School champions curiosity, excellence, impact and integrity, supported by strong global collaborations and a diverse, inclusive community. Be Yourself \u00a0 The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants \u00a0 We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. \u00a0\u00a0 Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Ting Xue (details in position description) For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that\u2019s shaping the future. Applications close: 1 November, 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.\u00a0 Position Description: \u00a0 JR-018338 Research Fellow in Mathematics_PD.pdf JR-018338 Research Fellow in Mathematics_PD.docx"}}, "rows": {"https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Casual-Academics---Melbourne-Dental-School_JR-017697-1": {"school_id": 1816, "posting_url": "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Casual-Academics---Melbourne-Dental-School_JR-017697-1", "job_title_in_post": "Casual Academics - Melbourne Dental School", "position_type": "Unclassified", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences", "area_key_words": "Doctor of Clinical Dentistry", "deadline_of_application": "", "position_start_date": "2026-08-21"}, "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Expressions-of-Interest--Indigenous-Employment-Opportunities-at-the-University-of-Melbourne_JR-006839": {"school_id": 1816, "posting_url": "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Expressions-of-Interest--Indigenous-Employment-Opportunities-at-the-University-of-Melbourne_JR-006839", "job_title_in_post": "Expressions of Interest